WEBVTT

1
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.280
Theatre – it can show us who we are,
who we were and who we could be.

2
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.520
It means imagining bigger,

3
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.400
understanding better,

4
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.720
feeling together –

5
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.520
on our stages and on tour,

6
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.360
in schools

7
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.080
and with communities,

8
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.600
on cinema screens worldwide

9
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.720
and now at home.

10
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.760
We are the National Theatre.

11
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.800
We make theatre for everyone.

12
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.080
Art helps us to see the world in new ways.

13
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.240
The stories behind great works
help to bring them to life,

14
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.320
and hearing them shouldn't be a privilege.

15
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.960
That's why we built Bloomberg Connects.

16
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.680
An app that lets you discover a growing
number of museums, gardens and parks,

17
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.040
behind-the-scenes interviews
and public art from around the world,

18
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.600
all in a single download
right in the palm of your hand.

19
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.000
Bloomberg Connects offers
a world of inspiration.

20
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.640
Join the National Theatre community
and hundreds of cultural institutions

21
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.960
to get a deeper look into the world of art
through Bloomberg Connects.

22
00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.000
(CONVERSATIONS, LAUGHTER)

23
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.000
[MAN HUMMING A JOLLY TUNE TO HIMSELF]

24
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.680
(CONVERSATIONS FADE)

25
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.160
[HUMMING CONTINUES]

26
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.200
[HE BEGINS TO WHISTLE THE TUNE]

27
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.720
[HE HUMS AGAIN]

28
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.200
[WHISTLING]

29
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.280
[HUMMING]

30
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.680
[WHISTLING]

31
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.200
[WELSH ACCENT]
This play is called 'Our Town'.

32
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.400
It was written by Thornton Wilder.

33
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.040
It's produced by David Sloane
and Pàdraig Cusack,

34
00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.920
and directed by Francesca Goodridge.

35
00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.840
In it, you will see Ms Roberts,

36
00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.520
Ms Reese-Williams,

37
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.680
Mr Meilir and Mr Trevannion,

38
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.360
also Ms Özdemir
and Mr Devlin and many others.

39
00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:16.240
The name of the town is Grover's Corners,

40
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.880
New Hampshire.

41
00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.120
Latitude 42 degrees, 40 minutes.

42
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.520
Longitude 70 degrees, 37 minutes.

43
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.640
The first act shows a day in our town.

44
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.200
– [BIRDSONG]
– The day is May 7th, 1901.

45
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.600
The time... is just before dawn.

46
00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.360
[COCK CROWS]

47
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.400
The sky is beginning to show some streaks
of light over in the east there,

48
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.200
behind our mountain.

49
00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.400
Ah, the morning star!

50
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.880
Always gets wonderful bright
the minute before it has to go.

51
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.480
Doesn't it?

52
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.760
Well, I'd better show you
how our town lies.

53
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.040
[♪ BRIGHT MELODY]

54
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.960
Up here is the main street, Stryd Fawr.

55
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.000
Way back there is the railway station.

56
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.160
[CLANG]

57
00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.240
– Tracks go that way.
– [CLANG]

58
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.480
Trebwyl is across the tracks.

59
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.880
Over there is the Congregational Church.

60
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.320
[BELL CLANGS]

61
00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.120
Across the street's the Presbyterian.

62
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.680
Methodist and Unitarian over there.

63
00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.680
Baptist is down in the valley,
by the river.

64
00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.120
Catholic Church...

65
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.840
..is over... beyond the tracks.

66
00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.400
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

67
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.760
Ah! Here's the Town Hall
and Post Office combined.

68
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.160
Jail's in the basement.

69
00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.680
Bevan once made a speech
from these very steps here.

70
00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.720
Along here's a row of stores.

71
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.880
Hitching posts and horse blocks
in front of them.

72
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.960
First automobile's gonna come along
in about five years.

73
00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.600
Belonged to Banker Cartwright,
our richest citizen.

74
00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.560
Lives in the big white house
up on the hill.

75
00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.720
Here's the grocery store.

76
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.640
– [BELL JANGLES]
– And here's Mr Morgan's drugstore.

77
00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:22.280
Most everybody in town manages
to look into these two stores...

78
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.520
– [BELLS JANGLE]
– ..once a day.

79
00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.760
Public school is over yonder.

80
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.840
High school, still further over.

81
00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.520
Quarter of nine mornings, noontimes,
and three o'clock afternoons,

82
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.120
the whole town can hear the yelling
and screaming

83
00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.880
– from those schoolyards.
– [CHILDREN'S LAUGHTER]

84
00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.000
– Woo!
– Woo-hoo!

85
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.800
Now, this is our doctor's house,

86
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.840
Doc Gibbs's.

87
00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.840
This is the back door.

88
00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.720
There's some scenery, for those who think
they have to have scenery.

89
00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.520
This is Mrs Gibbs's garden.

90
00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.400
Corn,

91
00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.480
peas,

92
00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:00.560
beans,

93
00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:01.600
hollyhocks,

94
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:02.800
heliotropes,

95
00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.040
and a lot of burdock.

96
00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.080
In those days,
our newspaper come out twice a week.

97
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:12.240
Yeah, the Grover's Corners Sentinel.

98
00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.760
And this is Editor Webb's house.

99
00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:17.880
This is Mrs Webb's garden.

100
00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.360
Just like Mrs Gibbs's,

101
00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.680
only it's got a lot of sunflowers, too.

102
00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:27.320
Right, here is...

103
00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.400
It's a big nut tree.

104
00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.600
Nice town, y'know what I mean?

105
00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.320
Nobody very remarkable
ever come out of it, so far as we know.

106
00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.680
Our earliest tombstones
in the cemetery up there,

107
00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.600
on the mountain, say 1670 to 1680.

108
00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.880
They're Grovers and Cartwrights,

109
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.120
Gibbses and Herseys.

110
00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.440
Same names as are around here now.

111
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.280
Well, as I said, it's about dawn.

112
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:09.520
The only lights on in town
are in a cottage over by the tracks

113
00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.800
where a Polish mother's just had twins.

114
00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.240
And in the Joe Crowell house,

115
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:18.400
where Joe Junior's getting up
so as to deliver the paper.

116
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:24.920
And in the depot, where Shorty Hawkins is
getting ready to flag the 5:45 for Boston.

117
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.520
[TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWS]

118
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.160
Naturally, out in the country, all around,

119
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.160
there've been lights on for some time,

120
00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.960
what with milkings and so on.

121
00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.800
But town people sleep late.

122
00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:40.720
So...

123
00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.320
..another day's begun.

124
00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.280
There's Doc Gibbs comin' down
Stryd Fawr now,

125
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.640
coming back from that baby case.

126
00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.120
And here's his wife,
coming downstairs to get breakfast.

127
00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.800
Doc Gibbs died in 1930.

128
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.240
The new hospital's named after him.

129
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.120
Mrs Gibbs died first –
long time ago, in fact.

130
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.240
She went out to visit
her daughter, Rebecca,

131
00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.960
who married an insurance man in Canton,

132
00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:12.920
and died there.

133
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.840
Pneumonia.

134
00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.680
But her body was brought back here.
She's up on the cemetery there now.

135
00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.040
In with a whole mess of Gibbses.

136
00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.040
And Herseys – she was Julia Hersey
'fore she married Doc Gibbs...

137
00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:32.720
..in the Congregational Church over there.

138
00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.000
In our town,
we like to know the facts about everybody.

139
00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.960
There's Mrs Webb, come downstairs
to get her breakfast, too.

140
00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.360
– [YAWN]
– Oh, that's Doc Gibbs.

141
00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.800
Got that call
at half past one this morning.

142
00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:50.200
– [WHISTLING]
– And there comes Joe Crowell Junior,

143
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.280
delivering Mr Webb's Sentinel.

144
00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.200
– Morning, Doc Gibbs.
– Morning, Joe.

145
00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.240
– Somebody been sick, Doc?
– No.

146
00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:59.680
– A pair of twins born over in Trebwyl.
– Do you want your paper now, or...?

147
00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.600
Yes, I'll take it. Anything serious
gone on in the world since Wednesday?

148
00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:04.800
Yes, sir.

149
00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.280
You know my schoolteacher, Miss Foster?

150
00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.480
Well, she's getting married
to a fella over in Concord.

151
00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.400
Well, I declare!
How do you boys feel about that?

152
00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.840
Well, of course, it's none of my business.

153
00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:20.240
But I think that if a person starts out
to be a teacher, she ought to stay one.

154
00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.280
DOC: Yeah. How's the knee, Joe?

155
00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:25.000
Oh, it's fine, Doc.
I never think about it at all.

156
00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:28.440
Only like you said,
it always tells me when it's gonna rain.

157
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.040
What's it telling you today? Gonna rain?

158
00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.120
– No, sir.
– Sure?

159
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:36.680
Yes, sir.

160
00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:39.080
– Knee ever make a mistake?
– No, sir.

161
00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:41.640
I want to tell you something
about that boy, Joe Crowell, there.

162
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.520
Joe was awful bright.

163
00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.880
He graduated from the high school here,
head of his class.

164
00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.680
So, he got a scholarship to the tech.

165
00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.760
Graduated head of his class there, too.

166
00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.560
It was all wrote up in the paper
at the time.

167
00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.880
Gonna be a great engineer, Joe was.

168
00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.360
But the war broke out
and he died in France.

169
00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.440
– All that education for nothing.
– [HORSE WHINNIES]

170
00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:07.360
MAN: Get up, Bessie!
What's the matter with you today?

171
00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.640
STAGE MANAGER: Here comes Howie Newsome,
deliverin' the milk.

172
00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.040
– Morning, Doc.
– Morning, Howie.

173
00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.440
– Somebody sick?
– Pair of twins over to Mrs Goruslawski's.

174
00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.680
Twins? Huh!
This town's getting bigger every year.

175
00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.120
– Gonna rain, Howie?
– No, no.

176
00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.880
– Fine day.
– Er...?

177
00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:24.600
Oh, that'll burn through!

178
00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:26.440
Come on, Bessie.

179
00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.680
DOC: Hello, Bessie.

180
00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:31.200
– How old is she, Howie?
– Going on 17.

181
00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.960
Bessie's all mixed up about the route

182
00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.440
ever since the Lockharts stopped
taking their quart of milk every day.

183
00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.440
She wants to leave 'em a quart just the
same, keeps scolding me the whole trip.

184
00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.160
– [CHUCKLES]
– Morning, Howie.

185
00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.640
Good morning, Mrs Gibbs.
Doc's just comin' down the street.

186
00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.960
– Is he? Seems like you're late today.
– Yep.

187
00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.240
Something went wrong with the separator.
Don't know what it was. Doc!

188
00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.480
– Howie!
– MRS GIBBS: Children!

189
00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.120
– Children! Time to get up.
– HOWIE: Come on, Bessie!

190
00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:54.960
George! Rebecca!

191
00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:59.560
– Everything all right, Frank?
– Yes, I declare! Easy as kittens.

192
00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:02.800
Bacon'll be ready in a minute.
Sit down and drink your coffee.

193
00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.720
You can catch a couple of hours' sleep
this morning, can't you?

194
00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:07.600
Mrs Wentworth's coming at 11.

195
00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.400
Guess I know what it's about, too.
Her stomach ain't what it ought to be.

196
00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.360
All told, you won't get more than
three hours' sleep.

197
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.440
Frank Gibbs,
I don't know what's gonna become of you.

198
00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:20.680
I do wish I could get you to go away
some place and take a rest.

199
00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.560
I think it'd do you good.

200
00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.920
Emily! Time to get up!
Wally! Seven o'clock!

201
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.760
MRS GIBBS: I declare,
you've got to speak to George.

202
00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.920
Seems like something's
come over him lately.

203
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.960
He's no help to me at all.
I can't even get him to chop me some wood.

204
00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:42.360
– Is he sassy to you?
– No, he just... whines.

205
00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.640
All he thinks about is that baseball.

206
00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.920
George! Rebecca!
You'll be late for school.

207
00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.080
– MRS GIBBS: George!
– George, look sharp!

208
00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:56.200
– Yes, Dad.
– Can't you hear your mother calling you?

209
00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.560
Guess I will go upstairs and get 40 winks.

210
00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.720
MRS WEBB: Wally! Emily!
You'll be late for school!

211
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.120
Wally, you wash yourself good
or I'll come up and do it myself.

212
00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.640
– Mam, what dress shall I wear?
– Don't make a noise.

213
00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.600
Your father's been out all night
and he needs his sleep.

214
00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:14.120
I've washed and ironed
the blue gingham for you special.

215
00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:16.520
Oh, Mam, I hate that dress.

216
00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:18.400
Oh, hush up with you.

217
00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.720
Every day I go to school
dressed like a sick turkey.

218
00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:27.520
– Now, Rebecca, you always look very nice.
– Mam, George is throwing soap at me.

219
00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:31.280
I'll come and slap the both of you –
that's what I'll do.

220
00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.760
– [FACTORY WHISTLE BLASTS]
– We've got a factory in our town, too.

221
00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.280
Hear it? Makes blankets.
Cartwrights own it.

222
00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:38.040
– It brung 'em a fortune.
– [LAUGHTER]

223
00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.080
MRS WEBB: Children! Now I won't have it.

224
00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:43.040
Breakfast is just as good
as any other meal

225
00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:45.720
and I won't have you gobbling like wolves.

226
00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:48.200
It'll stunt your growth – that's a fact.

227
00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:51.440
– Put away your book, Wally.
– Oh, Mam.

228
00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:54.200
By ten o'clock I got to know
all about Canada.

229
00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:58.920
You know the rule as well as I do –
no books at table.

230
00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:03.080
As for me, I'd rather have my children
healthy than bright.

231
00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:06.240
Well, I'm both, Mam. You know I am.

232
00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.120
I'm the brightest girl in school
for my age.

233
00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:11.240
I have a wonderful memory.

234
00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.400
MRS WEBB: Eat your breakfast!

235
00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:17.840
I'm bright, too,
when it comes to my stamp collection.

236
00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:19.960
I'll speak to your father about it
when he's rested.

237
00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:23.480
Seems to me 25 cents a week
is enough for a boy your age.

238
00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.040
I declare,
I don't know how you spend it all.

239
00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.280
Aw, Mam, I gotta lotta things to buy.

240
00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.640
Strawberry phosphates –
that's what you spend it on.

241
00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:35.720
I don't see how Rebecca comes to have
so much money. She has more than a dollar.

242
00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.560
I've been saving it up!

243
00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:39.440
Gradual.

244
00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:42.960
Well, I think it's a good thing
to spend some every now and then.

245
00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.640
Mam, do you know
what I love most in the world, do you?

246
00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:50.040
– Money!
– Eat your breakfast.

247
00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:52.080
– [BELL CLANGS]
– Mam, there's first bell.

248
00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:54.240
MRS WEBB: Walk fast,
but you don't have to run.

249
00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.480
Wally, pull up your pants at the knee.

250
00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:58.280
Stand up straight, Emily.

251
00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.880
MRS GIBBS: Tell Miss Foster
I send her my best congratulations.

252
00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.760
– Can you remember that?
– REBECCA: Yes, Mam.

253
00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.680
MRS GIBBS: You look real nice, Rebecca.
Pick up your feet.

254
00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.600
ALL: Goodbye.

255
00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.840
MRS GIBBS: Here, chick,
chick, chick, chick.

256
00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.440
Here, chick, chick, chick, chick, chick.

257
00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.920
[CLUCKING]

258
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.960
– No, go away, you.
– [SQUAWKING]

259
00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.520
Go away.

260
00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.800
What's the matter with you?
Fight, fight, fight, that's all you do.

261
00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:27.320
Oh, you don't belong to me.
Where did you come from?

262
00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.280
Don't be so scared.
No one's gonna hurt you.

263
00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:33.080
MRS GIBB: Good morning, Myrtle.
How's your cold?

264
00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:36.440
MRS WEBB: Well, I still get
that tickling feeling in my throat.

265
00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:40.360
I told Charles I didn't know
as I'd go to choir practice tonight.

266
00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:43.680
– Wouldn't be any use.
– Have you tried singing over your voice?

267
00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:48.280
Yes, but somehow I can't do that
and stay on the key.

268
00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:52.120
Eugh, while I'm resting myself
I thought I'd string some of these beans.

269
00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.680
MRS GIBBS: Oh, let me help you.
Beans have been good this year.

270
00:15:55.760 --> 00:16:00.000
I've decided to put up 40 quarts,
if it kills me.

271
00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.520
The children say they hate them,

272
00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:05.440
but I notice they're able
to get 'em down all winter.

273
00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.440
Myrtle, I've got to tell you something,

274
00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.080
because if I don't tell somebody
I'll burst.

275
00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:16.400
Why, Julia Gibbs!

276
00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:19.280
– Here, give me some more of those beans.
– [BOTH LAUGH]

277
00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:23.480
Myrtle,

278
00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:26.800
did one of those second-hand furniture men
come and see you last Friday?

279
00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.560
– No...?
– Well, he called on me, yeah.

280
00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.080
First, I thought he was a patient
wanting to see Dr Gibbs.

281
00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:37.440
And then he wormed his way
into my parlour, and, Myrtle Webb...

282
00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:43.880
he offered me $350 for Grandmother
Wentworth's highboy, as I'm sitting here!

283
00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:46.480
– Why, Julia Gibbs!
– He did!

284
00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.000
That old thing!

285
00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:50.240
Why, it was so big
I didn't even know where to put it,

286
00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.800
and I almost give it
to Cousin Hester Wilcox.

287
00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:55.520
Well, you're going to take it, aren't you?

288
00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:58.360
– I don't know.
– You don't know?

289
00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:02.920
– $350? What's come over you?
– Well...

290
00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.560
If I could get the doctor
to take the money

291
00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:10.440
and go away some place on a real trip,
I would sell it like that.

292
00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:18.280
You know, Myrtle, it's been the dream
of my life to see Paris, France.

293
00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:21.200
– [BOTH CHUCKLE]
– Oh, I don't know.

294
00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:22.840
It sounds crazy, I suppose,

295
00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:26.000
but for years I've been telling myself
that if we ever had the chance...

296
00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:29.200
– How does the doctor feel about it?
– Well...

297
00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:33.520
I did beat about the bush a little
and I said, 'If I ever got a legacy...'

298
00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.200
That's the way I put it.
I would make him take me somewhere.

299
00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.680
– And what did he say?
– You know how he is.

300
00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.720
I haven't heard a serious word out of him
since I've known him.

301
00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.000
'No,' he said.

302
00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:47.040
It might make him discontented
with Grover's Corners

303
00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:51.200
to go traipsin' about Europe.
'Better let well enough alone,' he says.

304
00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:56.200
Once every two years, he makes the trip
to the battlefields of the Civil War.

305
00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:59.240
'And that's enough treat for anybody,'
he says.

306
00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:01.440
– Ah!
– Mm-hm!

307
00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:03.880
Well, Mr Webb just admires the way

308
00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:06.880
Dr Gibbs knows everything
about the Civil War.

309
00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:13.360
Mr Webb's 'a good mind to give up Napoleon
and move over to the Civil War'.

310
00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:17.720
Only Dr Gibbs, being one of the greatest
experts in the country,

311
00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.480
just makes him despair.

312
00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.080
Well, it's a fact!

313
00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.800
Oh, the times I have walked
over those hills, Myrtle,

314
00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:29.240
stopping at every bush and pacing it out,
like we're gonna to buy it.

315
00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.480
Well, if that second-hand man
is really serious about buying it, Julia,

316
00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:35.840
you sell it, and then you'll get
to see Paris all right.

317
00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:40.080
Just keep dropping hints
from time to time.

318
00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.280
That's how I got to see
the Atlantic Ocean, you know.

319
00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:47.720
MRS GIBBS: Oh, I'm sorry I mentioned it.

320
00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:52.000
Only it seems to me
that once in your life, before you die,

321
00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:54.160
you ought to see a country
where they don't talk in English.

322
00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.040
– Yeah.
– And they don't even want to.

323
00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:58.120
– [MRS WEBB CHUCKLES]
– STAGE MANAGER: Thank you, ladies.

324
00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:02.600
Thank you very much.
Now, we are going to skip a few hours.

325
00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:06.800
But first, we want a little more
information about the town,

326
00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:09.960
kind of a scientific account,
you might say.

327
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:14.240
So, I have asked Professor Willard
of our university

328
00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:17.280
to sketch in a few details
of our past history here.

329
00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.000
Is Professor Willard here? May I introduce
Professor Willard of our university.

330
00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:27.360
A few brief notes, thank you, Professor.
Unfortunately, our time is limited.

331
00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:30.280
Oh, Grover's Corners, yes.

332
00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:32.240
Let me see, now.

333
00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.840
Grover's Corners lies
on the old Pleistocene granite

334
00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.720
of the Appalachian range.

335
00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.640
I may say, it's some of the oldest land
in the world.

336
00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.520
We're very proud of that.

337
00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:48.480
A shelf, right...
A shelf of Devonian basalt crosses it,

338
00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:53.440
with vestiges of Mesozoic shale,
and some sandstone outcroppings.

339
00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.520
But that's all more recent, you know.

340
00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:57.480
200... 300...

341
00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:59.240
million years old.

342
00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:02.680
Some highly interesting fossils
have been found –

343
00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:04.680
I may say unique fossils –

344
00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:08.840
two miles out of town,
in Silas Peckham's cow pasture.

345
00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:13.520
They can be seen at the museum
in our university at any time.

346
00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:17.240
That is, at any reasonable time.

347
00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:21.640
Shall I read some of Professor Gruber's
notes on the meteorological situation?

348
00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:25.760
– Mean precipitation, et cetera...
– Afraid we won't have time, Professor,

349
00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.880
but we might have a few words
about the history of Man here.

350
00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:32.960
Oh, yes, yes, anthropological data, yeah.

351
00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:37.600
Early Amerindian stock.
Cotahatchee tribes.

352
00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.480
No evidence
before the 10th century of this era.

353
00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.480
Now, entirely disappeared.

354
00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.520
Possible traces in three families.

355
00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:49.560
Migration towards the end
of the 17th century

356
00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:54.520
of English, yeah,
brachiocephalic blue-eyed stock.

357
00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.160
For the most part.

358
00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:58.800
Since then, some Slavs
and Mediterranean...

359
00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:03.640
– And the population, Professor Willard?
– Within the town limits, 2,640.

360
00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.240
Oh, just a moment, Professor...
[WHISPERS]

361
00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.240
Oh, yes, indeed?

362
00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:12.400
Oh, well,
the population at the moment is 2,642.

363
00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:16.000
The postal district brings in 507 more,

364
00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:19.920
making a total of 3,149.

365
00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:26.200
Mortality and birth rates constant –
by MacPherson's gauge, 6.032.

366
00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.040
Thank you very much, Professor. We're all
very much obliged to you, I'm sure.

367
00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:31.760
– Not at all, sir, not at all...
– This way, Professor.

368
00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:34.400
– Oh.
– And thank you again. Now...

369
00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:37.360
The political and social report,
Editor Webb.

370
00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:42.440
– Oh, Mr Webb?
– MRS WEBB: Er... Um...

371
00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.480
He'll be here in a minute.

372
00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:53.280
He just cut his hand
while he was eating an apple.

373
00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:59.000
– Thank you, Mrs Webb.
– Oh!

374
00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.440
Thank you.

375
00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:05.680
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

376
00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:08.080
MRS WEBB: Charles!

377
00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.600
[LOUD WHISPER] Everybody's waiting.

378
00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:15.600
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

379
00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.960
[CLEARS THROAT] Er, Mr Webb...

380
00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:22.800
is the, er, editor and publisher...

381
00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.840
..of the Grover's Corners Sentinel.

382
00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.200
That's our local paper...
You know. You know.

383
00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:36.080
– Oh!
– STAGE MANAGER: Ah!

384
00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.240
MR WEBB: Well, I don't have to tell you

385
00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:43.040
that we are run here
by a Board of Selectmen.

386
00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:45.840
All males vote at the age of 21.

387
00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:47.760
Women vote indirect.

388
00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:50.840
We are lower middle class.

389
00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:52.920
– Sprinkling of professional men.
– [MOUTHIBNG]

390
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.120
10% illiterate labourers.

391
00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:58.120
Politically, we are 86% Republicans,

392
00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.960
6% Democrats, 4% Socialists –
rest, indifferent.

393
00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:05.440
Religiously, we are 85% Protestants,

394
00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:06.960
12% Catholics,

395
00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:08.440
rest, indifferent.

396
00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.960
And have you any, er...
any comments, Mr Webb?

397
00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:16.760
Very ordinary town, if you ask me.

398
00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.560
Little better behaved than most.

399
00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:21.560
Probably a lot duller.

400
00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:23.120
[HE CHUCKLES]

401
00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:31.000
But our young people here
seem to like it well enough.

402
00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:36.160
90% of them graduating from high school
settle down right here to live.

403
00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.360
Even when they've been away to college.

404
00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:42.200
Now, is there anyone in the audience
who would like to ask Editor Webb

405
00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:44.320
– anything about the town?
– Yes.

406
00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:49.080
Yes. Is there much drinking
in Grover's Corners?

407
00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.560
Well, ma'am,
I wouldn't know what you'd call much.

408
00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:54.360
[HE CHUCKLES]

409
00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:00.960
Saturday nights, the farmhands meet
down in Ellery Greenough's stable

410
00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:04.160
and we've got one or two town drunks,

411
00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:08.800
but they're always having remorses
every time an evangelist comes to town.

412
00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:13.520
No, ma'am, I'd say liquor isn't
a regular thing in the home here,

413
00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:15.680
except in the medicine chest.

414
00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:19.080
Right good for snake bite, y'know?
Always was.

415
00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:23.360
– MAN: Is there no one in town aware...?
– Can you come forward, please?

416
00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:27.800
Down here, right to the front,
where we can all hear you.

417
00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:31.160
Yes, what were you saying?

418
00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:34.880
Is there no one in town aware
of any social injustice

419
00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:37.280
and industrial inequality?

420
00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:41.000
MR WEBB: Oh, yes, everybody is –
something terrible.

421
00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:45.760
Seems like they spend most of their time
talking about who's rich and who's poor.

422
00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:49.040
– Why don't they do something about it?
– Well, I...

423
00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:51.800
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

424
00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:54.560
Well, I dunno!

425
00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:59.440
I guess we are all hunting,
like everybody else,

426
00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:03.000
for a way the diligent and sensible
can rise to the top,

427
00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:06.120
and the lazy and quarrelsome
can sink to the bottom,

428
00:25:06.200 --> 00:25:08.080
but it's not easy to find.

429
00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:12.000
Meanwhile, we do all we can
to help those that can't help themselves,

430
00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.280
and those that can we leave alone.

431
00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:17.360
– Are there any other questions?
– WOMAN: Er, Mr Webb?

432
00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:19.360
Oh, haha, Mr Webb.

433
00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:25.280
Is there any culture
or love of beauty in Grover's Corners?

434
00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:27.880
– No, ma'am.
– (AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

435
00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:30.520
There isn't much,
not in the sense you mean.

436
00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:31.920
Come to think of it,

437
00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.880
there are some girls that play the piano
at high school commencement.

438
00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:38.720
But they are not happy about it.

439
00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:42.040
No, ma'am, there isn't much culture.

440
00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.720
But maybe this is the place to tell you

441
00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:49.120
that we've got a lot of pleasures
of a kind here.

442
00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:56.200
We like the sun coming up
over the mountain in the morning,

443
00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:59.320
and we all notice a good deal
about the birds.

444
00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.360
We pay a lot of attention to them.

445
00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:05.480
And we watch the change of the seasons.

446
00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:08.640
Yes, everybody knows about them.

447
00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:12.840
But those other things, ma'am,
you are right, there isn't much.

448
00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:16.560
Robinson Crusoe and the Bible.

449
00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:19.640
And Handel's 'Largo', we all know that.

450
00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:22.440
And 'Whistler's Mother'.

451
00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:26.200
– Those are just about as far as we go.
– WOMAN: That's what I thought.

452
00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:29.080
– Thank you, Mr Webb.
– Thank you, Mr Webb.

453
00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:31.880
Now, we'll go back to the town.

454
00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:34.560
– It's early afternoon.
– [BIRDSONG]

455
00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:40.800
All 2,642 have had their dinners
and all the dishes have been washed.

456
00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:45.800
There's an early-afternoon calm
in our town.

457
00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:49.280
A buzzing and a humming
from the school buildings.

458
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:52.560
Only a few buggies on Stryd Fawr.

459
00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:56.560
The horses dozing at the hitching posts –
you all remember what it's like.

460
00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.080
Doc Gibbs in his office.

461
00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:03.360
– Tapping people and making them say 'ah'.
– (AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

462
00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:06.320
Mr Webb, cutting his lawn over there.

463
00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:09.440
[LAUGHING] One man in ten

464
00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.680
thinks it's a privilege
to push his own lawnmower.

465
00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:16.360
– [CHILDREN'S LAUGHTER]
– No, sir. It's later than I thought.

466
00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:18.840
There's the children come home
from school already.

467
00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:23.840
No, I can't, Lois. I've got to go home
and help my mother. I promised.

468
00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:28.000
MR WEBB: Emily, walk simply.
Who do you think you are today?

469
00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:30.480
– [CHILDREN LAUGH]
– Dad!

470
00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:32.280
You're terrible.

471
00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:35.160
One minute
you tell me to stand up straight,

472
00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:37.800
and the next minute you call me names.

473
00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:40.840
But I just don't listen to you.

474
00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:43.520
– [KISS]
– Golly!

475
00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:48.240
I never got a kiss
from such a great lady before.

476
00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:50.400
Ah, 'scuse me, Mrs Forrest.

477
00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:53.360
[HIGH-PITCHED] Go out and play
in the fields, young man.

478
00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:56.040
You've got no business playing ball
on Stryd Fawr.

479
00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:57.800
I'm awfully sorry, Mrs Forrest.

480
00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:01.720
– Hello, Emily.
– Hello.

481
00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:05.600
– You made a fine speech in class.
– Well...

482
00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:09.600
I was really ready to make a speech
about the Monroe Doctrine,

483
00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:10.960
but at the last minute,

484
00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.960
Miss Corcoran made me talk
about the Louisiana Purchase instead.

485
00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:14.960
Ah.

486
00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:17.280
I worked an awful long time
on both of them.

487
00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:19.840
[HE CHUCKLES]
No, it's funny, um...

488
00:28:20.440 --> 00:28:22.680
Emily, from my window up there,

489
00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:26.480
I can just see your head nights when
you're doing your homework in your room.

490
00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:28.440
[LAUGHS] What?

491
00:28:28.520 --> 00:28:29.920
Can you?

492
00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.680
You certainly do stick to it, Emily.

493
00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:35.160
I don't see
how you can sit still that long.

494
00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.320
Guess you like school?

495
00:28:37.400 --> 00:28:40.120
Well, I always feel
it's something you have to go through.

496
00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:42.680
– Yeah.
– Oh, I don't mind it really.

497
00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:46.240
– It passes the time.
– Yeah. Emily, what do you think?

498
00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:48.400
We might work out a kind of a...

499
00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:52.040
telegraph from your window to mine,
and once in a while,

500
00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:55.560
you could give me a kinda hint or two
about one of those algebra problems.

501
00:28:58.480 --> 00:28:59.920
GEORGE: Hm?

502
00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.720
I don't mean the answers, Emily,
of course not, just some little hint.

503
00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:06.800
Oh, I think hints are allowed.

504
00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:12.160
So, er, if you get stuck, George, whistle
to me and I'll give you some hints.

505
00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:16.000
Emily!
You're just naturally bright, I guess.

506
00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:18.600
I figure that it's just the way
a person's born.

507
00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:21.680
Yeah.

508
00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.320
But, you see, I want to be a farmer,

509
00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:28.680
and my Uncle Luke says whenever I'm ready
I can come over and work on his farm,

510
00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:32.400
and if I'm any good...
I can just gradually have it.

511
00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:35.800
You mean the house and everything?

512
00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:39.080
– Yeah.
– MRS WEBB: A-hem!

513
00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:43.840
Well, thanks. I'd better be getting out
to the field. Thanks for the talk, Emily.

514
00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:47.320
– Good afternoon, Mrs Webb.
– Good afternoon, George.

515
00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:49.680
– So long, Emily.
– So long, George.

516
00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:53.440
MRS WEBB: Emily, come and help me
string these beans for the winter.

517
00:29:53.520 --> 00:29:58.320
George Gibbs let himself have
a real conversation, didn't he?

518
00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:00.480
Why, he's growing up.

519
00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:02.480
How old would George be?

520
00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:04.440
– I dunno.
– Let's see.

521
00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:07.200
He must be almost 16.

522
00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:09.400
– Mam!
– [CHUCKLES]

523
00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:13.080
I made a speech in class today
and I was very good.

524
00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:17.040
You must recite it to your father
at supper. What was it about?

525
00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:19.560
The Louisiana Purchase.

526
00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:22.920
It was like silk off a spool.

527
00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:27.360
I'm going to make speeches all my life.

528
00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:29.640
[SIGHS]

529
00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:33.920
Mam, are these big enough?

530
00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:37.040
Try and get them a little bigger,
if you can.

531
00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.080
Mam, will you answer me
a question, serious?

532
00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:43.960
'Seriously', dear, not 'serious'.

533
00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:47.040
– Seriously... will you?
– Of course I will.

534
00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:49.400
Mam, am I good-looking?

535
00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:54.600
Yes. Of course you are.

536
00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:59.200
All my children have got good features.
I'd be ashamed if they hadn't.

537
00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:05.120
Oh, Mam, that's not what I mean.
What I mean is... am I pretty?

538
00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:09.240
I've already told you, yes.
Now, that's enough of that.

539
00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:15.440
You have a nice young pretty face.
Ah! I never heard of such foolishness.

540
00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:18.320
Mam, you never tell us the truth
about anything.

541
00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:21.800
– I am telling you the truth.
– Mam, were you pretty?

542
00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:25.560
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

543
00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:27.480
Yes!

544
00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.120
I was.

545
00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.200
If I do say it.

546
00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:35.320
I was the prettiest girl in town.

547
00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:38.840
Yeah, next to Mamie Cartwright.

548
00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:42.600
But, Mam, you've got to say
something about me.

549
00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:44.520
Am I pretty enough to get anybody...

550
00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.200
..to get people interested in me?

551
00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:50.680
Emily, you make me tired, now stop it.

552
00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:54.480
You are pretty enough
for all normal purposes.

553
00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:57.720
Come along now
and bring that bowl with you.

554
00:31:57.800 --> 00:32:00.480
– Oh, Mam! You are no help at all.
– Come on.

555
00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:02.720
– [THEY CONTINUE SQUABBLING]
– Thank you, that'll do.

556
00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:05.000
We have to interrupt again here.
Thank you, Mrs Webb. Thank you, Emily.

557
00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:08.200
Now, there are some more things
we want to explore about this town.

558
00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:10.840
I think this is a good... Ah, come on!

559
00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:13.080
I think this is a good time

560
00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:15.240
to tell you that the Cartwright interests

561
00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:18.160
have just begun building a new bank
in Grover's Corners.

562
00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:22.120
Yeah, had to go to Vermont for the marble,
sorry to say,

563
00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:27.160
and they've asked a friend of mine
what they should put in the cornerstone.

564
00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:31.280
You know, for people to dig up
1,000 years from now.

565
00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:34.840
Of course, they've put in a copy
of The Times.

566
00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:38.480
And a copy of Mr Webb's Sentinel.

567
00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:41.600
We're kind of interested in this
because some scientific fellas

568
00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:45.960
have found a way of painting
all that reading matter with a glue –

569
00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:48.080
a silicate glue –

570
00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:51.920
that'll make it keep,
ooh, 1,000, 2,000 years.

571
00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:53.640
[CICADAS SINGING]

572
00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:56.600
We're putting in a Bible.

573
00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:01.200
The Constitution of the United States.

574
00:33:02.920 --> 00:33:05.440
And a copy of William Shakespeare's plays.

575
00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:09.440
What do you say, folks?

576
00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:11.440
Huh?

577
00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:15.800
– Well, what do you think?
– (AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

578
00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:20.040
(LAUGHTER)

579
00:33:21.520 --> 00:33:28.360
You know, Babylon once had...
ooh, two million people in it?

580
00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:31.880
And all we know about 'em
is the names of the kings.

581
00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.960
And some copies of wheat contracts.

582
00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:38.400
Oh, and contracts for the sale of slaves.

583
00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:43.560
Yet, every night,
all those families sat down to supper,

584
00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:46.360
and the father came home from his work,

585
00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:48.960
and the smoke went up the chimney,
same as here.

586
00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:52.440
And even in Greece and Rome,

587
00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:55.040
all we know about the real life
of the people

588
00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:57.360
is what we can piece together
out of the joking poems

589
00:33:57.440 --> 00:34:00.280
and the comedies they wrote
for the theatre back then.

590
00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:07.240
So, I'm going to have a copy of this play
put in the cornerstone.

591
00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:12.800
And people 1,000 years from now'll know
a few simple facts about us.

592
00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:19.440
Not just the Treaty of Versailles
or the Lindbergh flight.

593
00:34:19.520 --> 00:34:21.480
You see what I mean?

594
00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:22.920
So...

595
00:34:24.280 --> 00:34:27.360
[ADDRESSING THE FUTURE]
..people 1,000 years from now...

596
00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.440
..this is the way we were...

597
00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:33.880
at the beginning of the 20th century.

598
00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:37.440
This is the way we were.

599
00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:41.080
In our growing up

600
00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:43.840
and in our marrying

601
00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:45.480
and in our living...

602
00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:49.840
..and in our dying.

603
00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:56.960
– Well... good deal of time's gone by.
– [♪ PIANO BEGINS]

604
00:34:57.960 --> 00:34:59.520
It's evening.

605
00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:04.120
♪ Mae Un sy'n ein tynnu ynghyd... ♪

606
00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:07.760
You can hear choir practice going on
in the Congregational Church.

607
00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:11.400
The children are at home
doing their schoolwork.

608
00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:15.720
The day's running down like a tired clock.

609
00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:21.600
[SOPRANO SINGS FORCEFULLY]
♪ ..i foli ei enw Ef ♪

610
00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:27.160
Now, look here, everybody.

611
00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:30.600
Music come into the world
to give pleasure.

612
00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:33.400
Softer.

613
00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:35.200
Softer!

614
00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:39.200
Get it out of your heads
that music's only good when it's loud.

615
00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:42.840
Leave loudness to the Methodists.

616
00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:47.840
You couldn't beat 'em,
even if you wanted to.

617
00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:49.840
Now again.
– [♪ PIANO STARTS]

618
00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:51.120
Tenors!

619
00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:59.280
♪ Mae Un sy'n ein tynnu ynghyd

620
00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:04.000
♪ I brofi holl gariad y Nef... ♪

621
00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:05.640
GEORGE: Psst! Emily!

622
00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:07.480
– Hello.
– Hello.

623
00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:12.040
I can't work at all.
The moonlight's so terrible.

624
00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:15.320
– Emily, did you get the third problem?
– Which?

625
00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:17.080
Er, the third?

626
00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:19.880
[♪ SINGING CONTINUES SOFTLY]

627
00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:23.360
Why, yes, George.
That's the easiest of them all.

628
00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:26.040
I don't see it.

629
00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:29.640
Emily, can you give me a hint?

630
00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:32.040
Look, I'll tell you one thing.

631
00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:34.480
The answer's in yards.

632
00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:36.120
In yards?

633
00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:43.880
How do you mean?

634
00:36:43.960 --> 00:36:45.520
In square yards.

635
00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:48.560
– Oh... in square yards.
– Yes!

636
00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:50.480
George, don't you see?

637
00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:53.120
Y-yeah.

638
00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:57.000
In square yards...

639
00:36:58.280 --> 00:37:00.120
..of wallpaper.

640
00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.000
Wallpaper!

641
00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:06.600
Oh, I see. Thanks a lot, Emily.

642
00:37:06.680 --> 00:37:08.600
You're welcome.

643
00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:13.000
My, isn't the moonlight terrible?

644
00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:16.640
And choir practice going on.

645
00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:20.800
I think if you hold your breath you can
hear the train all the way to Coed y Cnau.

646
00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:22.240
Oh.

647
00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:31.080
EMILY: Hear it?

648
00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:33.360
[DISTANT TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWS]

649
00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:37.360
Mm-hm. Cor, what do you know!

650
00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:45.320
Well, I should probably get back
and try to work.

651
00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:48.840
Good night, Emily, and... thanks.

652
00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:52.320
Good night, George.

653
00:37:52.400 --> 00:37:56.520
Before I forget it, how many of you
can come in Tuesday afternoon?

654
00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:01.720
And sing at Fred Hersey's wedding?

655
00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:03.360
Show your hands.

656
00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:08.680
[GROANS] That'll be fine.

657
00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:11.120
That'll be right nice.

658
00:38:11.200 --> 00:38:16.000
We'll do the same music we did
for Jane Trowbridge last month.

659
00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:20.040
Now we'll do
'Wyt ti'n llwythog a blinderog?'

660
00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:24.560
'Art Thou Weary? Art Thou Languid?'

661
00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:32.320
It's a question, ladies and gentlemen.
Make it talk.

662
00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:37.240
– Ready.
– [♪ PIANO BEGINS]

663
00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:43.480
♪ Wyt ti'n llwythog a blinderog?... ♪

664
00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:46.280
DR GIBBS: George!
Will you come down a minute?

665
00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.480
GEORGE: Yes, Dad.

666
00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:57.680
♪ Un a ddywed,
"Tyred ataf, Gorffwys mwy" ♪

667
00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:01.120
DR GIBBS: Make yourself comfortable,
George, I'll only keep you a minute.

668
00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:04.240
George, how old are you?

669
00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:07.600
I'm 16. Almost 17.

670
00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:09.600
What do you want to do
after school's over?

671
00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:11.480
Why, you know, Dad.

672
00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:13.960
I want to be a farmer
on Uncle Luke's farm.

673
00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:18.520
You'll be willing, will you, to get up
early and milk and feed the stock?

674
00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.320
And you'll be able to hoe and hay all day?

675
00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:23.440
Sure, I will. What are you...?

676
00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:25.720
– What do you mean, Dad?
– Well, George...

677
00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:29.920
While I was in my office today
I heard a funny sound.

678
00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.240
What do you think it was?

679
00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:34.160
It was your mother.

680
00:39:34.240 --> 00:39:36.320
Chopping wood.

681
00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:37.920
There you see your mother.

682
00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:41.320
Getting up early, cooking meals
all day long, washing, ironing

683
00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:45.760
and still, she has to go out
in the backyard and chop wood.

684
00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:48.800
I suppose she just got tired
of asking you.

685
00:39:48.880 --> 00:39:51.760
She just gave up and decided
it would be easier to do it herself.

686
00:39:51.840 --> 00:39:55.240
And you eat her food, put on
the clothes she keeps nice for you,

687
00:39:55.320 --> 00:39:59.440
and run off and play ball, like she's some
hired girl we keep around the house

688
00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:01.840
but that we don't like very much.

689
00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:04.320
[SNIFFLES]

690
00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:08.400
Well, I knew all I had to do
was bring your attention to it.

691
00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:11.200
[CRYING]

692
00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:17.280
Here's a handkerchief, son.

693
00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:20.680
George, I've decided to raise
your spending money.

694
00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:23.800
25 cents a week – not, of course,
for chopping wood for your mother,

695
00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:28.000
because that's a present you give to her –
but because you are getting older

696
00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:31.360
and I imagine there are lots of things
you must find to do with it, hm?

697
00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:32.760
Thanks, Dad.

698
00:40:32.840 --> 00:40:34.360
Now, let's see.

699
00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:36.560
Tomorrow's your payday.

700
00:40:36.640 --> 00:40:38.520
You can count on it, hm?

701
00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:42.280
Ah, probably Rebecca'll feel
she ought to have more now, too.

702
00:40:45.160 --> 00:40:47.400
Whatever could have happened
to your mother?

703
00:40:48.080 --> 00:40:50.760
Choir practice never was this late before.

704
00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:53.120
It's only half past eight, Dad.

705
00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.760
I don't know why she's in that old choir.

706
00:40:56.840 --> 00:40:59.600
Hasn't got any more voice
than an old crow.

707
00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:01.480
[GEORGE LAUGHS]

708
00:41:01.560 --> 00:41:05.000
Traipsin' around the streets
at this hour of the night!

709
00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:11.400
Just about time you retired,
don't you think?

710
00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:13.560
Yes, Dad. Oh...

711
00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:19.160
WOMAN: Good night, Martha.
Good night, Mr Foster.

712
00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:23.200
MRS WEBB: I'll tell Mr Webb.
I know he'll want to put it in the paper.

713
00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:25.520
– My, it's late!
– Good night, Irma.

714
00:41:25.600 --> 00:41:28.720
MRS GIBBS: Real nice choir practice,
wasn't it?

715
00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:34.440
Oh, Myrtle Webb!
Look at that moon, will you?

716
00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:37.880
– Potato weather, for sure.
– [BOTH CHUCKLE]

717
00:41:37.960 --> 00:41:42.440
A-hem! Naturally, I didn't want to say
a word about it in front of those others,

718
00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:44.840
but now that we're alone, huh, really?

719
00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:48.320
It is the worst scandal
that ever was in this town!

720
00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:50.560
– What?
– Simon Stimson!

721
00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.440
– MRS GIBBS: Now, Louella...
– But, Julia!

722
00:41:52.520 --> 00:41:57.440
To have the organist of a church drink
and drunk, year after year.

723
00:41:57.520 --> 00:42:00.040
– You know he was drunk tonight.
– Now, Louella!

724
00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:04.440
We all know about Mr Stimson
and we all know about the...

725
00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:08.920
..the troubles that he's been through,
and Dr Ferguson knows it, too.

726
00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:11.600
If Dr Ferguson decides
to keep him on there in that job

727
00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:16.560
the only thing the rest of us can do
is just... not to notice it.

728
00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:19.600
Not to notice it? But it's getting worse.

729
00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:23.960
No, it isn't, Louella.
It's getting better.

730
00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:26.960
I've been in that choir
twice as long as you have.

731
00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:29.920
It doesn't happen anywhere near so often.

732
00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.000
Ah, my!

733
00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:36.160
I hate to go to bed on a night like this.

734
00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:39.720
[SIGHS] Oh, I'd better hurry.

735
00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:43.760
Those children will be sitting up
until all hours.

736
00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:51.280
Good night, Louella.

737
00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:57.040
– Can you get home safe, Louella?
– It's as bright as day.

738
00:42:57.120 --> 00:42:59.960
I can see Mr Soames scowling
at the window now.

739
00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:03.600
You'd think we'd been to a dance,
the way menfolk carry on.

740
00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:12.120
– Well, we had a real good time.
– You're late enough.

741
00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:14.240
Why, Frank, it's no later than usual.

742
00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:17.280
And you stopping at the corner
to gossip with a lot of hens.

743
00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:19.800
Oh, now, Frank, don't be grouchy.

744
00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:26.840
Come out
and smell the heliotrope in the moonlight.

745
00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:30.160
[SHE CLICKS HER TONGUE]

746
00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:36.760
[SHE BREATHES IN DEEPLY]

747
00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:42.760
Ah! Isn't that wonderful?

748
00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:47.200
What did you do all the time I was away?

749
00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:52.280
Oh, I read, as usual. What were the girls
gossiping about tonight?

750
00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:54.960
Believe me, Frank,
there's something to gossip about.

751
00:43:55.040 --> 00:43:56.920
Simon Stimson far gone, was he?

752
00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:59.120
MRS GIBBS: Worst I've ever seen him.

753
00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:04.240
How will that end, Frank?
Dr Ferguson can't forgive him for ever.

754
00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:07.280
I suppose I know more
about Simon Stimson's affairs

755
00:44:07.360 --> 00:44:09.600
than anyone in this town.

756
00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:12.920
Some people aren't made
for small-town life.

757
00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:17.120
I don't know how that'll end but there's
nothing we can do but just leave it alone.

758
00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:19.760
– Come, get in.
– No...

759
00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:22.080
Not yet.

760
00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:34.040
– Frank, I'm worried about you.
– What are you worried about?

761
00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:39.600
I think it's my duty to make plans
for you to get a real rest and change.

762
00:44:39.680 --> 00:44:43.440
And if I get that legacy,
well, I am going to insist on it.

763
00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:46.080
Julia, there's no sense
in going over this again.

764
00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:49.520
– Frank, you're just unreasonable!
– Come on, Julia!

765
00:44:50.800 --> 00:44:54.480
It's getting late.
First thing you know you'll catch cold.

766
00:44:55.040 --> 00:44:58.080
I gave George a piece of my mind tonight.

767
00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:01.720
I reckon you'll have your wood chopped
for a while, anyway.

768
00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:03.680
No, no, start getting upstairs...

769
00:45:03.760 --> 00:45:07.880
Oh, dear, there's just so many things
to pick up, seems like.

770
00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:15.920
You know, Frank, Mrs Fairchild always
locks her front door every night.

771
00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:18.360
All those people up that part of town do.

772
00:45:18.440 --> 00:45:21.480
They're getting citified,
that's the trouble with them.

773
00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:24.240
They got nothing fit to burgle
and everybody knows it.

774
00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:26.880
GEORGE: Get out, Rebecca!

775
00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:29.960
There's only room for one at this window.
You're always spoiling everything.

776
00:45:30.040 --> 00:45:32.240
Well, let me look just a minute.

777
00:45:32.320 --> 00:45:35.560
– Use your own window.
– I did, but there's no moon there.

778
00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:37.600
BOTH: Ah... AGH!

779
00:45:39.160 --> 00:45:43.320
Oh, George, do you want to know
what I think, do you?

780
00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:49.720
I think maybe the moon
is getting nearer and nearer.

781
00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:53.360
And there'll be a big 'splosion.

782
00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:56.200
[SCOFFS] Rebecca, you don't know anything.

783
00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:02.800
If the moon were getting nearer,

784
00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:06.400
the guys that sit up all night
with telescopes would see it first,

785
00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:09.960
and they'd tell about it,
and it'd be in all the newspapers.

786
00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:19.440
George, is the moon shining
on South America,

787
00:46:19.520 --> 00:46:22.720
Canada and half the whole world?

788
00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:26.160
Well... probably is.

789
00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:27.840
[REBECCA CHUCKLES]

790
00:46:28.880 --> 00:46:31.800
[♪ SIMPLE MELANCHOLIC PIANO MELODY
OVER TENSE TONES]

791
00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:23.880
[♪ MELODY RISES]

792
00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:45.240
[♪ FADES]

793
00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:48.120
STAGE MANAGER: 9:30.

794
00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:49.560
[BIRDSONG]

795
00:47:53.240 --> 00:47:55.600
– Most of the lights are out.
– [OWL HOOTS]

796
00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:59.400
No, there's Constable Warren trying
a few doors on Stryd Fawr.

797
00:47:59.480 --> 00:48:03.840
And here comes Editor Webb,
after putting his newspaper to bed.

798
00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:09.800
MR WEBB: Good evening, Bill.

799
00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:12.880
– Evenin', Mr Webb.
– Quite a moon!

800
00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:15.560
– Yeah.
– All quiet tonight?

801
00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:20.640
Simon Stimson's been...
rolling around a little.

802
00:48:20.720 --> 00:48:24.880
Just saw his wife moving out to hunt
for him, so I looked the other way.

803
00:48:24.960 --> 00:48:27.120
Oh, here he comes now.

804
00:48:27.880 --> 00:48:30.080
MR WEBB: Good evening, Simon.

805
00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:33.120
Town seems to have settled down
for the night pretty well.

806
00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:35.480
Good evening!

807
00:48:36.800 --> 00:48:38.680
Yes.

808
00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:41.600
Most of the town has settled down
for the night, Simon.

809
00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:43.840
I guess we'd better do the same.

810
00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:48.160
Can I walk along ways with you?

811
00:48:56.080 --> 00:48:58.480
I don't know how that's gonna end,
Mr Webb.

812
00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:01.880
Well, he's seen a peck of trouble,
one thing after another.

813
00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:03.160
Ooh, Bill.

814
00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:08.240
If you see my boy smoking cigarettes,
give him a word, will you?

815
00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:09.800
He thinks a lot of you, Bill.

816
00:49:09.880 --> 00:49:12.720
I don't think he smokes no cigarettes,
Mr Webb.

817
00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:15.200
Leastways, no more than two or three.

818
00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:18.800
A year.

819
00:49:18.880 --> 00:49:22.480
Yes. Well, good night, Bill.

820
00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:24.360
Good night, Mr Webb.

821
00:49:26.000 --> 00:49:29.000
MR WEBB: Who's that up there?
Is that you, Myrtle?

822
00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:31.080
No, it's me, Dad.

823
00:49:31.160 --> 00:49:33.280
Why aren't you in bed?

824
00:49:34.600 --> 00:49:36.440
I dunno.

825
00:49:36.520 --> 00:49:42.040
I just can't sleep yet, Dad.
The moonlight's so... wonderful.

826
00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:45.920
Oh, and the smell of Mrs Gibbs's
heliotrope, can you smell it?

827
00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:47.360
Hm. Yes.

828
00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:50.000
Haven't any troubles on your mind,
have you, Emily?

829
00:49:50.080 --> 00:49:52.240
Troubles, Dad? No.

830
00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:57.400
Well, enjoy yourself,
but don't let your mother catch you.

831
00:49:58.640 --> 00:50:00.800
– Good night, Emily.
– Good night, Dad.

832
00:50:01.360 --> 00:50:04.360
REBECCA: Oh, George, I never told you

833
00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:09.280
about the letter Jane Crofut got
from her minister when she was sick.

834
00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:11.640
He wrote Jane a letter,

835
00:50:11.720 --> 00:50:15.360
and on the envelope,
the address was like this.

836
00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:18.720
It said,
'Jane Crofut,

837
00:50:18.800 --> 00:50:20.840
'The Crofut Farm,

838
00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:22.640
'Grover's Corners,

839
00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:24.000
'Sir Swddon,

840
00:50:24.080 --> 00:50:26.160
– 'New Hampshire.'
– What's funny about that?

841
00:50:26.240 --> 00:50:28.760
But listen, it's not finished.

842
00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:33.680
'Continent of North America,

843
00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:36.240
'Western Hemisphere,

844
00:50:36.320 --> 00:50:37.960
'the Earth,

845
00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:40.200
'the Solar System,

846
00:50:40.280 --> 00:50:42.160
'the Universe,

847
00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:44.040
'the Mind of God.'

848
00:50:44.120 --> 00:50:45.720
[BOTH LAUGH]

849
00:50:45.800 --> 00:50:48.880
– That's what it said on the envelope!
– Cor, what do you know?

850
00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:52.880
And the postman brought it, just the same!

851
00:50:54.280 --> 00:50:56.440
Well, what do you know?!

852
00:50:57.240 --> 00:51:00.840
[♪ FULL, UPLIFTING MELODY]

853
00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:03.520
Rebecca, look!

854
00:51:17.360 --> 00:51:19.680
[BOTH LAUGHING]

855
00:52:01.920 --> 00:52:03.520
[♪ FADES]

856
00:52:06.760 --> 00:52:08.760
Three years have gone by.

857
00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:12.960
Yes, the sun's come up over 1,000 times.

858
00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:17.680
Summers and winters have cracked
the mountains a little bit more,

859
00:52:17.760 --> 00:52:20.800
and the rains have brought down
some of the dirt.

860
00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:23.240
Some babies that weren't even born before

861
00:52:23.320 --> 00:52:26.040
have begun talking
regular sentences already.

862
00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:29.840
And a number of people
who thought they were right young and spry

863
00:52:29.920 --> 00:52:34.280
have noticed that they can't bound up
a flight of stairs like they used to,

864
00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:36.960
without their heart fluttering a little.

865
00:52:37.040 --> 00:52:39.240
All that can happen in 1,000 days.

866
00:52:39.320 --> 00:52:42.880
Nature has been pushing
and contriving in other ways, too.

867
00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:48.000
A number of young people fell in love
and got married.

868
00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:50.440
Yes!

869
00:52:50.520 --> 00:52:53.680
The mountain got bit away
a few fractions of an inch,

870
00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:56.360
millions of gallons of water
went by the mill

871
00:52:56.440 --> 00:53:01.120
and, here and there,
a new home was set up under a roof.

872
00:53:02.800 --> 00:53:06.480
Almost everybody in the world
gets married.

873
00:53:07.600 --> 00:53:09.200
You know what I mean?

874
00:53:09.280 --> 00:53:12.120
In our town,
there aren't hardly any exceptions.

875
00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:16.400
Most everybody in the world
climbs into their graves married.

876
00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:19.920
The first act was called 'Daily Life'.

877
00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:24.120
This act is called 'Love and Marriage'.

878
00:53:25.080 --> 00:53:27.960
There's another act coming after this.

879
00:53:28.040 --> 00:53:30.160
I reckon you can guess what that's about.

880
00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:34.400
– So, it's three years later, it's 1904.
– [THUNDER RUMBLING]

881
00:53:34.480 --> 00:53:37.480
It's July 7th,
just after high school commencement.

882
00:53:37.560 --> 00:53:40.760
That's the time most of our young people
jump up and get married.

883
00:53:40.840 --> 00:53:43.240
Soon as they've passed
their last examinations

884
00:53:43.320 --> 00:53:46.240
in solid geometry
or Cicero's Orations,

885
00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:49.400
looks like they suddenly feel themselves
fit to be married.

886
00:53:49.480 --> 00:53:51.360
Well, it's early morning.

887
00:53:52.040 --> 00:53:55.360
Only this time, it's been raining.

888
00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:59.240
Oh, God, it's been pouring and thundering.

889
00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:02.720
Mrs Gibbs's garden,
and Mrs Webb's here, drenched.

890
00:54:02.800 --> 00:54:05.640
All those bean poles
and pea vines, drenched.

891
00:54:05.720 --> 00:54:08.200
All yesterday over there on Stryd Fawr

892
00:54:08.280 --> 00:54:12.520
the rain looked like curtains
being blown along.

893
00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:13.960
Ha!

894
00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:15.480
It may begin again.

895
00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:18.360
[THUNDER CRACK]

896
00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:23.840
– Any minute.
– [TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWS]

897
00:54:23.920 --> 00:54:27.200
There!
You can hear the 5:45 for Boston.

898
00:54:28.160 --> 00:54:33.960
And there's Mrs Gibbs and Mrs Webb
come downstairs to make breakfast,

899
00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:36.080
just as though it were an ordinary day.

900
00:54:36.160 --> 00:54:39.240
Now, I don't have to point out
to the women in my audience

901
00:54:39.320 --> 00:54:42.080
that those ladies they see before them,
both those ladies,

902
00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:44.960
cooked three meals a day!

903
00:54:45.040 --> 00:54:48.560
One of 'em for 20 years, the other for 40!

904
00:54:48.640 --> 00:54:50.720
And no summer vacation.

905
00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:54.520
They brought up two children apiece,
washed, cleaned the house –

906
00:54:54.600 --> 00:54:56.960
and never a nervous breakdown.

907
00:54:58.200 --> 00:55:01.160
It's like what one of those
Middle West poets said,

908
00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:05.640
'You've got to love life to have life.

909
00:55:08.200 --> 00:55:11.760
'And you've got to have life
to love life.'

910
00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:14.160
It's what they call a vicious circle.

911
00:55:14.240 --> 00:55:15.840
– [HORSE WHINNIES]
– Giddap, Bessie!

912
00:55:15.920 --> 00:55:18.120
Here comes Howie Newsome
delivering the milk.

913
00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:23.080
And here's Si Crowell delivering
the papers, like his brother before him.

914
00:55:23.960 --> 00:55:25.960
– Morning, Howie.
– Morning, Si.

915
00:55:26.040 --> 00:55:29.040
– Anything in the paper I ought to know?
– Nothing much.

916
00:55:29.120 --> 00:55:34.080
Except we're losing about the best
ball player Grover's Corners ever had!

917
00:55:35.640 --> 00:55:37.520
– George Gibbs.
– Reckon he is.

918
00:55:37.600 --> 00:55:41.960
– He could hit and run bases, too.
– Yeah, a mighty fine ball player.

919
00:55:42.040 --> 00:55:45.240
Whoa! Bessie! I guess I can stop and talk
if I've a mind to!

920
00:55:46.360 --> 00:55:52.040
I just don't see how he could give up
a thing like that, just to get married.

921
00:55:52.120 --> 00:55:54.640
– Would you, Howie?
– Can't tell, Si.

922
00:55:54.720 --> 00:55:57.400
– Never had no talent that way.
– CONSTABLE WARREN: Morning!

923
00:55:57.480 --> 00:55:59.760
HOWIE: Morning, Bill. You're up early.

924
00:55:59.840 --> 00:56:02.280
Seeing if there's anything I can do
to prevent a flood.

925
00:56:02.360 --> 00:56:04.080
River's been risin' all night.

926
00:56:04.160 --> 00:56:08.360
Si Crowell's all worked up here about
George Gibbs retiring from playing ball.

927
00:56:08.440 --> 00:56:11.160
CONSTABLE WARREN: Yes, sir,
that's the way it goes.

928
00:56:11.240 --> 00:56:15.200
Back in '84, Si, we had this player,
even George Gibbs couldn't touch him.

929
00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:19.160
Name of Hank Todd.
Went down to Maine and became a parson!

930
00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:22.960
Wonderful ball player.
Howie, how does the weather look to you?

931
00:56:23.040 --> 00:56:25.560
It ain't bad.
Think maybe it'll clear up for good.

932
00:56:25.640 --> 00:56:27.720
– [THUNDER RUMBLES]
– MRS GIBBS: Good morning, Howie.

933
00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:30.000
– Do you think it's going to rain again?
– Morning, Mrs Gibbs.

934
00:56:30.080 --> 00:56:31.840
It rained so heavy, maybe it'll clear up.

935
00:56:31.920 --> 00:56:34.120
– I certainly hope it will.
– How much did you want today?

936
00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:37.080
I'm going to have a houseful
of relations, Howie.

937
00:56:37.160 --> 00:56:39.800
Looks to me like I'll need
three-a-milk and two-a-cream.

938
00:56:40.800 --> 00:56:44.120
My wife says to tell you we both hope
they'll be very happy, Mrs Gibbs.

939
00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:46.000
– Know they will.
– Thanks a lot, Howie.

940
00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:48.480
And tell your wife
I hope she gets there to the wedding.

941
00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:50.640
Yes, she'll be there.
She'll be there if she can.

942
00:56:50.720 --> 00:56:53.760
– Good morning, Mr Newsome.
– Good morning, Mrs Webb.

943
00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:57.080
I told you four quarts of milk,
but I hope you can spare me another.

944
00:56:57.160 --> 00:56:59.280
– Yes, and the two of cream.
– [THUNDER RUMBLES]

945
00:56:59.360 --> 00:57:01.560
Will it start raining again, Mr Newsome?

946
00:57:01.640 --> 00:57:04.920
I was just saying to Mrs Gibbs
as to how it may lighten up.

947
00:57:05.000 --> 00:57:08.320
Mrs Newsome told me to tell you we both
hope they'll be very happy, Mrs Webb.

948
00:57:08.400 --> 00:57:10.320
– Know they will.
– Thank you.

949
00:57:10.400 --> 00:57:14.040
And thank Mrs Newsome, and we're
counting on seeing you at the wedding.

950
00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:16.920
We hope to get there. Couldn't miss that.

951
00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:20.360
– Come on, Bessie.
– DR GIBBS: Well, Ma, the day has come.

952
00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:21.960
You're losin' one of your chicks.

953
00:57:22.040 --> 00:57:24.360
Frank Gibbs, don't you say another word.

954
00:57:24.440 --> 00:57:28.200
I feel like crying every minute.
Sit down and drink your coffee.

955
00:57:28.280 --> 00:57:31.520
The groom's up shaving himself,
only there ain't an awful lot to shave.

956
00:57:32.200 --> 00:57:35.160
Whistling and singing,
like he's glad to leave us.

957
00:57:35.240 --> 00:57:38.480
Every now and then,
he says 'I do' to the mirror.

958
00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:42.680
– It don't sound very convincing to me.
– Oh, I declare, Frank.

959
00:57:42.760 --> 00:57:44.640
I don't know how he'll get along.

960
00:57:44.720 --> 00:57:48.760
I've arranged his clothes and I've seen
to it that he's put warm things on.

961
00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:52.560
Oh, Frank, they're too young!
Emily won't think of such things.

962
00:57:52.640 --> 00:57:55.120
He will catch his death of cold
within a week.

963
00:57:55.200 --> 00:57:57.480
I was remembering
my wedding morning, Julia.

964
00:57:57.560 --> 00:57:59.960
Now, don't start that, Frank Gibbs!

965
00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:02.600
I was the scaredest young fella
in the state.

966
00:58:02.680 --> 00:58:04.880
I thought I'd made a mistake for sure.

967
00:58:04.960 --> 00:58:08.240
But when I saw you coming down that aisle

968
00:58:08.320 --> 00:58:11.360
I thought you were
the prettiest girl I ever saw.

969
00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:15.760
Only, the trouble was,
I'd never... 'seen you' before.

970
00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:19.680
There I was in the Congregational Church
marrying a total stranger.

971
00:58:19.760 --> 00:58:21.600
And how do you think I felt?

972
00:58:21.680 --> 00:58:25.320
Oh, Frank,
weddings are perfectly awful things!

973
00:58:26.240 --> 00:58:28.000
Farces, that's what they are.

974
00:58:28.800 --> 00:58:31.720
Here, I've made something for you.

975
00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:37.120
Why, Julia Hersey – French toast!

976
00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:39.720
Wasn't hard to make
and I had to do something.

977
00:58:39.800 --> 00:58:41.400
How'd you sleep last night, Julia?

978
00:58:41.480 --> 00:58:45.000
Well, I heard
a lot of the hours struck off.

979
00:58:45.080 --> 00:58:49.360
I get a shock every time I think
of George setting out to be a family man –

980
00:58:49.440 --> 00:58:51.680
that great gangling thing!

981
00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:56.680
I tell you, Julia, there's nothing
so terrifying in this world as a son.

982
00:58:56.760 --> 00:59:00.000
The relation of father and son
is the darndest, awkwardest...

983
00:59:00.080 --> 00:59:02.520
Well, mother and daughter's no picnic,
let me tell you.

984
00:59:03.560 --> 00:59:05.560
DR GIBBS: They'll have their struggles,
I suppose,

985
00:59:05.640 --> 00:59:07.600
but that's none of our business.

986
00:59:08.560 --> 00:59:11.200
Everyone has a right
to their own struggles.

987
00:59:11.760 --> 00:59:12.720
Yes.

988
00:59:13.720 --> 00:59:16.920
People are meant to go through life
two-by-two.

989
00:59:17.600 --> 00:59:19.920
It ain't natural to be lonesome.

990
00:59:21.560 --> 00:59:24.160
Do you know one of the things
I was scared of when I married you?

991
00:59:24.240 --> 00:59:25.560
Oh, go along with you!

992
00:59:25.640 --> 00:59:27.920
I was afraid we'd have material
for conversation

993
00:59:28.000 --> 00:59:29.880
no more than would last a few weeks.

994
00:59:29.960 --> 00:59:33.080
– [LAUGHS] I was afraid we'd run out!
– [SHE SHRIEKS]

995
00:59:33.160 --> 00:59:36.720
And eat our meals in silence.

996
00:59:36.800 --> 00:59:40.160
Well, you and I been conversing
for 20 years now

997
00:59:40.240 --> 00:59:42.640
without any noticeable barren spells.

998
00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:46.000
Well, good weather, bad weather.

999
00:59:47.040 --> 00:59:50.040
Ain't very choice,
but I always find something to say.

1000
00:59:50.120 --> 00:59:54.520
Did you hear Rebecca
stirring around upstairs?

1001
00:59:54.600 --> 00:59:58.800
No. Only day of the year she hasn't been
managing everyone's business up there.

1002
00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:02.120
She's hiding in her room.
I get the impression she's crying.

1003
01:00:02.200 --> 01:00:04.120
Lord's sakes! This has got to stop.

1004
01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:05.440
Rebecca!

1005
01:00:07.200 --> 01:00:09.200
Rebecca, come and get your breakfast.

1006
01:00:09.280 --> 01:00:12.440
Good morning, everybody.
Only five more hours to live.

1007
01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:14.960
MRS GIBBS: George Gibbs,
where are you going?

1008
01:00:15.040 --> 01:00:16.920
Just stepping across the grass
to see my girl.

1009
01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:19.840
Now, George, you put on your overshoes.
It's raining torrents.

1010
01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:22.880
You do not go out of this house
without you're prepared for it.

1011
01:00:22.960 --> 01:00:24.120
Mam, it's just a step!

1012
01:00:24.200 --> 01:00:27.120
George, you will catch your death of cold
and cough all through the service.

1013
01:00:27.200 --> 01:00:29.320
George, do as your mother tells you!

1014
01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:33.680
From tomorrow on,
you can kill yourself in all weathers,

1015
01:00:33.760 --> 01:00:35.960
but while you're in my house
you will live wisely, thank you.

1016
01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:41.360
And maybe, Mrs Webb isn't used
to callers at seven in the morning.

1017
01:00:41.440 --> 01:00:44.160
– Here, take a cup of coffee first....
– I'll be back in a minute.

1018
01:00:49.760 --> 01:00:54.080
– Good morning, Mother Webb.
– [SCREAMS] Goodness! You frightened me!

1019
01:00:54.160 --> 01:00:57.720
Now, George,
you can come in a minute out of the wet,

1020
01:00:57.800 --> 01:01:00.800
– but you know I can't ask you in.
– Why not?

1021
01:01:00.880 --> 01:01:03.520
George, you know as well as I do,

1022
01:01:03.600 --> 01:01:06.320
the groom can't see his bride
on his wedding day.

1023
01:01:06.400 --> 01:01:08.960
Not until he sees her in church.

1024
01:01:09.040 --> 01:01:10.720
That's just a superstition.

1025
01:01:10.800 --> 01:01:13.360
– Good morning, Mr Webb.
– Good morning, George.

1026
01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:16.560
Now, Mr Webb, you don't believe
in that superstition do you?

1027
01:01:16.640 --> 01:01:19.840
There's a lot of common sense
in some superstitions, George.

1028
01:01:19.920 --> 01:01:22.080
Millions have followed it, George,

1029
01:01:22.160 --> 01:01:26.800
and you don't want to be the first
to fly in the face of custom.

1030
01:01:27.920 --> 01:01:30.440
– [SHE SIGHS]
– How's Emily?

1031
01:01:30.520 --> 01:01:34.080
She hasn't waked up yet.
I haven't heard a sound out of her.

1032
01:01:34.160 --> 01:01:36.040
– Emily's asleep?
– Ssh!

1033
01:01:36.720 --> 01:01:38.520
No wonder!

1034
01:01:38.600 --> 01:01:40.560
We were up until all hours.

1035
01:01:42.200 --> 01:01:44.840
Sewing and packing.

1036
01:01:50.200 --> 01:01:52.520
Now, look, I'll tell you what I'll do.

1037
01:01:52.600 --> 01:01:58.760
You sit down here a minute with Mr Webb
and drink this cup of coffee.

1038
01:01:58.840 --> 01:02:03.200
And I'll go upstairs and see
she doesn't come down and surprise you.

1039
01:02:04.440 --> 01:02:06.320
There's some bacon, too.

1040
01:02:07.440 --> 01:02:09.640
But don't be long about it.

1041
01:02:13.480 --> 01:02:16.520
[THEY LAUGH AWKWARDLY]

1042
01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:38.880
– [LOUDLY] Well, George!
– [LAUGHS NERVOUSLY]

1043
01:02:38.960 --> 01:02:40.920
– How are you?
– Oh, fine.

1044
01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:42.640
Yeah, I'm fine.

1045
01:02:42.720 --> 01:02:45.680
Mr Webb, what sense could there be
in a superstition like that?

1046
01:02:45.760 --> 01:02:52.280
Well, you see, on her wedding morning
a girl's head's apt to be full of...

1047
01:02:55.680 --> 01:02:58.640
..clothes and one thing and another.

1048
01:02:58.720 --> 01:03:01.480
– Don't you think that's probably it?
– Yes, I never thought of that.

1049
01:03:01.560 --> 01:03:04.560
A girl's apt to be a mite nervous
on her wedding day, George.

1050
01:03:04.640 --> 01:03:07.760
I wish a fellow could get married
without all that marching up and down.

1051
01:03:07.840 --> 01:03:11.880
Every man that's ever lived
has felt that way about it, George,

1052
01:03:11.960 --> 01:03:13.960
but it hasn't been any use.

1053
01:03:14.040 --> 01:03:17.520
It's the womenfolk
who have built up weddings, my boy.

1054
01:03:17.600 --> 01:03:21.160
For a while now,
the women have it all their own.

1055
01:03:21.240 --> 01:03:24.800
A man looks pretty small at a wedding,
George.

1056
01:03:25.760 --> 01:03:28.920
All those good women
standing shoulder-to-shoulder,

1057
01:03:29.000 --> 01:03:33.200
making sure that the knot is tied
in a mighty public way.

1058
01:03:33.280 --> 01:03:35.560
GEORGE: But you do believe in it,
don't you, Mr Webb?

1059
01:03:35.640 --> 01:03:39.280
Oh, yes, don't you misunderstand me,
my boy, marriage is a wonderful thing.

1060
01:03:39.360 --> 01:03:41.000
Wonderful thing!

1061
01:03:41.080 --> 01:03:43.840
– And don't you forget that, George.
– No, sir.

1062
01:03:47.880 --> 01:03:51.360
Mr Webb, how old were you
when you got married?

1063
01:03:51.440 --> 01:03:54.200
Well, you see, I'd been to college

1064
01:03:54.280 --> 01:03:58.320
and I'd taken a little time
to, um, get settled.

1065
01:03:58.400 --> 01:04:01.760
But Mrs Webb, well, she wasn't much older
than what Emily is and...

1066
01:04:03.760 --> 01:04:10.440
Age hasn't much to do with it, George,
not compared with, um... other things.

1067
01:04:10.520 --> 01:04:12.760
– What were you going to say, Mr Webb?
– I don't know.

1068
01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:14.840
Was I going to say something?

1069
01:04:18.520 --> 01:04:22.600
George, I was thinking the other night

1070
01:04:22.680 --> 01:04:26.400
of some advice my father gave me
when I got married.

1071
01:04:27.560 --> 01:04:30.160
'Charles,' he said.

1072
01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:35.080
'Charles, start out early
showing who's boss,' he said.

1073
01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:39.960
'Best thing to do is to give an order,
even if it don't make sense,

1074
01:04:40.040 --> 01:04:42.120
'just so she'll learn to obey.

1075
01:04:42.200 --> 01:04:46.120
'And,' he said, 'if anything
about your wife irritates you –

1076
01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:49.960
'her conversation, anything –
just get up and leave the house.'

1077
01:04:51.320 --> 01:04:54.120
'That will make it clear to her,' he said.

1078
01:04:54.200 --> 01:04:55.440
And, oh, yes!

1079
01:04:55.520 --> 01:05:01.680
He said, 'Never, never let your wife know
how much money you have, never.'

1080
01:05:03.200 --> 01:05:05.880
Well, Mr Webb, I don't think I could...

1081
01:05:05.960 --> 01:05:10.920
So, I took the opposite of my father's
advice and I've been happy ever since!

1082
01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:12.800
[GEORGE LAUGHS]

1083
01:05:14.160 --> 01:05:19.160
Let that be a lesson to you, George,
never to ask advice on personal matters.

1084
01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:24.520
George, are you going to raise chickens
on your farm?

1085
01:05:24.600 --> 01:05:25.880
What?

1086
01:05:25.960 --> 01:05:28.600
Are you going to raise chickens
on your farm?

1087
01:05:28.680 --> 01:05:32.000
– My Uncle Luke's nev...
– A book came into my office the other day

1088
01:05:32.080 --> 01:05:34.760
on the Philo System of raising chickens.

1089
01:05:34.840 --> 01:05:36.080
I want you to read it.

1090
01:05:36.160 --> 01:05:38.800
I'm thinking of beginning in a small way
in the backyard,

1091
01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:41.040
and I'm going to put an incubator
in the cellar...

1092
01:05:41.120 --> 01:05:46.360
Charles! Are you talking
about that old incubator again?

1093
01:05:46.440 --> 01:05:49.400
I thought you two would be talking
about things worthwhile.

1094
01:05:49.480 --> 01:05:52.520
Well, Myrtle, if you want to give the boy
some good advice,

1095
01:05:52.600 --> 01:05:55.160
I'll go upstairs
and leave you alone with him.

1096
01:05:59.640 --> 01:06:01.440
George,

1097
01:06:01.520 --> 01:06:04.200
Emily's got to come down
and eat her breakfast.

1098
01:06:04.280 --> 01:06:08.800
She sends you her love, but she doesn't
want to lay eyes on you. Goodbye.

1099
01:06:09.640 --> 01:06:11.760
– Goodbye.
– [SHE GROANS]

1100
01:06:13.280 --> 01:06:17.080
Myrtle, I guess you don't know
about that older superstition.

1101
01:06:17.160 --> 01:06:20.640
– What do you mean, Charles?
– Since the cavemen.

1102
01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:25.160
No bridegroom should see his father-in-law
on the day of the wedding, or near it.

1103
01:06:25.240 --> 01:06:26.480
Now, you remember that.

1104
01:06:26.560 --> 01:06:28.400
STAGE MANAGER: Thank you very much,
Mr and Mrs Webb.

1105
01:06:28.480 --> 01:06:31.960
I have to interrupt again here. You see,
we want to know how all this began.

1106
01:06:32.040 --> 01:06:38.040
This wedding.
This plan to spend a lifetime together.

1107
01:06:38.760 --> 01:06:42.040
I'm awfully interested
in how big things like that begin.

1108
01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:46.280
You know how it is, you're 21 or 22
and you make some decisions.

1109
01:06:46.360 --> 01:06:48.200
Then, whoosh, you're 70!

1110
01:06:49.840 --> 01:06:52.000
You've been a lawyer for 50 years,

1111
01:06:52.080 --> 01:06:57.480
and that white-haired lady at your side
has eaten over 50,000 meals with you!

1112
01:06:57.560 --> 01:06:59.840
How do such things begin?

1113
01:07:00.720 --> 01:07:05.080
George and Emily are going to show you now
the conversation that they had

1114
01:07:05.160 --> 01:07:07.760
when they first knew that, er...

1115
01:07:07.840 --> 01:07:13.160
that, well, as the saying goes,
they were meant for one another.

1116
01:07:14.800 --> 01:07:18.720
But before they do it, I want you to try
and remember what it was like

1117
01:07:18.800 --> 01:07:20.640
to have been very young.

1118
01:07:21.640 --> 01:07:25.840
And particularly those days
when you were first in love.

1119
01:07:26.280 --> 01:07:30.160
Oh! When you were like a person
sleepwalking.

1120
01:07:31.040 --> 01:07:33.800
And you didn't quite see
the street you were in.

1121
01:07:34.600 --> 01:07:39.000
And you didn't quite hear everything
that was said to you.

1122
01:07:39.080 --> 01:07:42.880
You're just a little bit crazy.
Will you remember that, please?

1123
01:07:42.960 --> 01:07:45.800
Now, they'll be coming out
of school at three o'clock.

1124
01:07:45.880 --> 01:07:49.120
George has just been elected
President of the Junior Class.

1125
01:07:49.200 --> 01:07:53.400
As it's June, it means he'll be President
of the Senior Class all next year.

1126
01:07:53.480 --> 01:07:58.920
And Emily has just been elected
Secretary and Treasurer.

1127
01:07:59.960 --> 01:08:01.880
I don't have to tell you
how important that is.

1128
01:08:01.960 --> 01:08:04.520
Yep, here they are
coming down Stryd Fawr now.

1129
01:08:04.600 --> 01:08:06.040
EMILY: I can't, Louise.

1130
01:08:06.120 --> 01:08:08.240
I've got to go home. Goodbye.

1131
01:08:08.320 --> 01:08:10.320
Oh, Ernestine! Ernestine.

1132
01:08:10.400 --> 01:08:12.480
Can you come over tonight and do Latin?

1133
01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:15.280
– Isn't that Cicero the worst thing?
– [SHE MOUTHS]

1134
01:08:15.360 --> 01:08:17.600
– Tell your mother you have to. G'bye.
– Bye!

1135
01:08:17.680 --> 01:08:19.240
G'bye, Helen. G'bye, Fred.

1136
01:08:19.320 --> 01:08:21.440
Can I carry your books home for you,
Emily?

1137
01:08:21.520 --> 01:08:23.560
[COOLY] No, thank you. It isn't far.

1138
01:08:24.480 --> 01:08:26.000
Er, excuse me a minute, Emily.

1139
01:08:26.080 --> 01:08:29.280
Say, Bob, if I'm a little bit late,
start practice anyway.

1140
01:08:29.360 --> 01:08:31.640
And give Herb some long high ones.

1141
01:08:31.720 --> 01:08:33.680
EMILY: Goodbye, Lizzy.

1142
01:08:33.760 --> 01:08:35.400
Oh, goodbye, Lizzy.

1143
01:08:38.880 --> 01:08:40.960
[HE CHUCKLES NERVOUSLY]

1144
01:08:41.040 --> 01:08:43.560
I'm awfully glad
you got elected, too, Emily.

1145
01:08:43.640 --> 01:08:45.080
Thank you.

1146
01:08:46.480 --> 01:08:48.400
Emily, why are you mad at me?

1147
01:08:48.480 --> 01:08:52.160
– I'm not mad at you.
– You've been treating me so funny lately.

1148
01:08:52.240 --> 01:08:57.040
Well, since you ask me,
I might as well say it right out, George.

1149
01:08:57.120 --> 01:08:59.960
EMILY: Ooh! Goodbye, Miss Corcoran.

1150
01:09:02.200 --> 01:09:03.920
GEORGE: Goodbye, Miss Corcoran.

1151
01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:10.840
GEORGE: Wha... What is it?

1152
01:09:12.520 --> 01:09:16.760
I don't like the whole change
that's come over you in the last year.

1153
01:09:16.840 --> 01:09:18.680
I'm sorry if that hurts your feelings,

1154
01:09:18.760 --> 01:09:21.360
but I've got to tell the truth
and shame the devil.

1155
01:09:21.440 --> 01:09:23.520
A change? Wha...?

1156
01:09:23.600 --> 01:09:25.160
What do you mean?

1157
01:09:26.320 --> 01:09:29.080
Well, up to a year ago,
I used to like you.

1158
01:09:30.480 --> 01:09:31.800
A lot.

1159
01:09:31.880 --> 01:09:34.320
And I used to watch you
as you did everything

1160
01:09:34.400 --> 01:09:36.560
because we'd been friends so long.

1161
01:09:36.640 --> 01:09:40.200
And then you began spending
all your time playing ball

1162
01:09:40.280 --> 01:09:43.240
and you never stopped
to speak to anybody any more.

1163
01:09:44.120 --> 01:09:46.760
Not even to your own family you didn't.

1164
01:09:46.840 --> 01:09:48.640
And, George, it's a fact,

1165
01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:51.560
you've got awful conceited and stuck-up,
and all the girls say so.

1166
01:09:53.360 --> 01:09:54.880
They may not say so to your face,

1167
01:09:54.960 --> 01:09:57.040
but that's what they say about you
behind your back,

1168
01:09:57.120 --> 01:10:00.680
and it hurts me to hear them say it,
but I've got to agree with them a little.

1169
01:10:03.120 --> 01:10:06.720
I'm sorry if it hurts your feelings.

1170
01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:10.720
But I can't be sorry I said it.

1171
01:10:12.200 --> 01:10:13.480
GEORGE: I...

1172
01:10:13.560 --> 01:10:15.480
I'm glad you said it, Emily.

1173
01:10:16.240 --> 01:10:19.000
I never thought that such a thing
was happening to me.

1174
01:10:20.160 --> 01:10:24.080
I guess it's hard for a fella not to have
his faults creep into his character.

1175
01:10:26.000 --> 01:10:30.440
I always expect a man to be perfect,
and I think he should be.

1176
01:10:30.520 --> 01:10:33.760
Oh... I don't think it's possible
to be perfect, Emily.

1177
01:10:33.840 --> 01:10:37.000
Well, my father is,
and as far as I can see your father is.

1178
01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:39.240
There's no reason on Earth
why you shouldn't be, too.

1179
01:10:39.320 --> 01:10:41.200
Well, I think it's the other way round,
see?

1180
01:10:41.280 --> 01:10:44.000
That men aren't naturally good,
but girls are.

1181
01:10:45.160 --> 01:10:50.360
Well, you might as well know right now
that I'm not perfect.

1182
01:10:52.840 --> 01:10:55.920
It's not as easy for a girl
to be as perfect as a man,

1183
01:10:56.000 --> 01:10:58.880
because we girls are more...

1184
01:10:59.920 --> 01:11:02.000
..more...

1185
01:11:05.560 --> 01:11:06.960
..nervous.

1186
01:11:08.920 --> 01:11:12.440
Now I'm sorry I said all that about you.
I don't know what made me say it.

1187
01:11:12.520 --> 01:11:14.880
– Emily...
– Now I can see it's not the truth at all,

1188
01:11:14.960 --> 01:11:17.280
and I suddenly feel it isn't important,
anyway.

1189
01:11:17.360 --> 01:11:18.680
Emily!

1190
01:11:18.760 --> 01:11:22.400
Would you like an ice-cream soda,
or something, before you go home?

1191
01:11:22.480 --> 01:11:25.040
Well, thank you. I... I would.

1192
01:11:25.120 --> 01:11:27.320
GEORGE: Good afternoon, Stew. How are you?

1193
01:11:27.400 --> 01:11:29.160
Good afternoon, Mrs Slocum.

1194
01:11:29.240 --> 01:11:31.360
STAGE MANAGER: Hello, George!

1195
01:11:31.440 --> 01:11:33.800
Hello, Emily. What'll you have?

1196
01:11:33.880 --> 01:11:38.680
Why, Emily Webb,
what have you been crying about?

1197
01:11:40.720 --> 01:11:42.520
Ah, she...

1198
01:11:42.600 --> 01:11:46.160
She just had an awful scare, Mr Morgan.

1199
01:11:46.240 --> 01:11:48.720
She nearly got run over
by that hardware-store wagon.

1200
01:11:48.800 --> 01:11:52.600
Everybody says that Tom Huckins
drives like a crazy man.

1201
01:11:52.680 --> 01:11:55.280
Well, now,
you take a drink of water, Emily.

1202
01:11:56.200 --> 01:11:58.080
You look all shook-up.

1203
01:11:58.160 --> 01:12:02.560
I tell you what, you've got to look both
ways when you cross Stryd Fawr these days.

1204
01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:04.640
Gets worse every year.
What'll you have?

1205
01:12:04.720 --> 01:12:06.800
I'll have a strawberry phosphate,
thank you, Mr Morgan.

1206
01:12:06.880 --> 01:12:09.240
No, Emily, have an ice-cream soda with me.

1207
01:12:09.320 --> 01:12:12.640
Two strawberry ice-cream sodas, Mr Morgan.

1208
01:12:13.800 --> 01:12:17.560
Two strawberry ice-cream sodas?!

1209
01:12:19.160 --> 01:12:21.320
– Yes, sir!
– [THEY LAUGH]

1210
01:12:21.400 --> 01:12:23.120
Yes, sir!

1211
01:12:28.040 --> 01:12:35.040
There are 125 horses in Grover's Corners
this minute I'm talking to you.

1212
01:12:37.200 --> 01:12:39.240
State Inspector was in here yesterday.

1213
01:12:41.440 --> 01:12:45.040
And now they're bringing in
these automobiles.

1214
01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:48.440
Now, best thing you can do
is just stay home.

1215
01:12:48.520 --> 01:12:54.880
Why, I can remember when a dog could go to
sleep all day in the middle of Stryd Fawr

1216
01:12:54.960 --> 01:12:58.720
and nothing come along to disturb him.
There you are. Enjoy 'em. Yes, Mrs Ellis.

1217
01:12:58.800 --> 01:13:00.760
What can I do for you?

1218
01:13:05.920 --> 01:13:07.600
[SLURPING]

1219
01:13:08.520 --> 01:13:11.440
EMILY: Ah! They're so...

1220
01:13:11.520 --> 01:13:14.440
– expensive.
– No, no, Emily. Don't think of that.

1221
01:13:14.520 --> 01:13:18.400
We're celebrating our election. And then
do you know what else I'm celebrating?

1222
01:13:18.480 --> 01:13:19.480
No!

1223
01:13:19.560 --> 01:13:21.520
I'm celebrating
that I've got a great friend

1224
01:13:21.600 --> 01:13:24.880
who tells me all the things
that ought to be told me.

1225
01:13:25.960 --> 01:13:30.760
George, please don't think of that.
I don't know why I said it. It's not true.

1226
01:13:30.840 --> 01:13:34.000
No, Emily, I'm glad you spoke to me
like you did, but you'll see,

1227
01:13:34.080 --> 01:13:38.360
I am gonna change so quick.
You bet I'm gonna change.

1228
01:13:39.920 --> 01:13:42.320
– And, Emily, I want to ask you a favour.
– What?

1229
01:13:42.400 --> 01:13:46.520
Emily, if I go away
to agriculture college next year...

1230
01:13:48.360 --> 01:13:50.640
..will you write me a letter
once in a while?

1231
01:13:51.560 --> 01:13:53.280
I certainly will.

1232
01:13:54.800 --> 01:13:57.280
I certainly will, George.

1233
01:13:59.240 --> 01:14:00.600
[SLURPING]

1234
01:14:01.960 --> 01:14:07.400
It certainly seems like being away three
years, you'd get out of touch with things.

1235
01:14:08.320 --> 01:14:12.960
Maybe letters from Grover's Corners
wouldn't be so interesting after a while.

1236
01:14:14.040 --> 01:14:18.120
Grover's Corners isn't a very important
place when you think of all New Hampshire.

1237
01:14:19.360 --> 01:14:21.240
But I think it's a very nice town.

1238
01:14:21.320 --> 01:14:24.960
The day wouldn't come when I wouldn't want
to know everything that's happening here.

1239
01:14:25.040 --> 01:14:26.720
I know that's true, Emily.

1240
01:14:27.320 --> 01:14:29.640
Well...

1241
01:14:29.720 --> 01:14:32.160
I'll try to make my letters interesting.

1242
01:14:35.240 --> 01:14:36.840
You know, Emily,

1243
01:14:36.920 --> 01:14:39.800
whenever I meet a farmer, I always ask him

1244
01:14:39.880 --> 01:14:42.480
if he thinks it's important
to go to agriculture school

1245
01:14:42.560 --> 01:14:44.160
– to be a good farmer.
– Why...

1246
01:14:44.240 --> 01:14:46.680
And some of them say
that it's even a waste of time.

1247
01:14:46.760 --> 01:14:51.320
You can get all those things, anyway, out
of the pamphlets the government sends out.

1248
01:14:51.400 --> 01:14:52.960
Uncle Luke's getting old now.

1249
01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:56.000
He's about ready for me to start
taking over the farm tomorrow, if I could.

1250
01:14:56.080 --> 01:14:58.920
– My...!
– Like you say, being gone all that time,

1251
01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:01.520
in other places, meeting other people.

1252
01:15:01.600 --> 01:15:04.680
Gosh, if anything like that can happen,
I don't want to go away.

1253
01:15:06.680 --> 01:15:09.560
I guess new people
aren't any better than old ones.

1254
01:15:09.640 --> 01:15:13.080
I bet they almost never are. Emily, I feel
like you're as good a friend as I've got.

1255
01:15:14.560 --> 01:15:16.880
I don't need to go and meet
the people in other towns.

1256
01:15:16.960 --> 01:15:18.920
But, George,

1257
01:15:19.000 --> 01:15:23.320
maybe it's very important for you
to go and learn all that about...

1258
01:15:23.400 --> 01:15:27.360
cattle judging and soils and those things,
of course, I don't know.

1259
01:15:27.440 --> 01:15:30.320
Emily, I'm going to make up my mind
right now.

1260
01:15:31.480 --> 01:15:32.480
I won't go.

1261
01:15:33.840 --> 01:15:35.800
– I'll tell Dad tonight...
– Why, George,

1262
01:15:35.880 --> 01:15:39.080
I don't see why you have to decide
right now – it's a whole year away.

1263
01:15:39.160 --> 01:15:44.600
Emily, I'm glad you spoke to me
about that... fault in my character.

1264
01:15:44.680 --> 01:15:48.160
What you said was right,
but there was one thing wrong in it,

1265
01:15:48.240 --> 01:15:52.280
and that was when you said that for a year
I haven't been noticing people. And you.

1266
01:15:53.800 --> 01:15:55.560
For instance.

1267
01:15:55.640 --> 01:15:58.480
Why, you say you were watching me
when I did everything,

1268
01:15:58.560 --> 01:16:00.800
I was doing the same about you
all the time.

1269
01:16:01.480 --> 01:16:05.200
Why, sure, I always thought about you as
one of the chief people I thought about.

1270
01:16:06.120 --> 01:16:08.640
I always made sure
where you were sitting on the bleachers,

1271
01:16:08.720 --> 01:16:13.440
and who you were with, and for three days
I have been trying to walk home with you.

1272
01:16:13.520 --> 01:16:15.240
Something always got in the way.

1273
01:16:15.320 --> 01:16:17.800
Yesterday, I was standing over there
by the wall waiting for you,

1274
01:16:17.880 --> 01:16:20.320
– and you walked home with Miss Corcoran.
– George!

1275
01:16:21.760 --> 01:16:24.480
Life's awful funny!
How could I have known that...?

1276
01:16:24.560 --> 01:16:28.080
Listen, Emily, I'm gonna tell you
why I'm not going to agriculture school.

1277
01:16:29.240 --> 01:16:31.480
I think that...

1278
01:16:31.560 --> 01:16:34.960
when you've found a person...

1279
01:16:35.040 --> 01:16:37.560
that you are very fond of...

1280
01:16:37.640 --> 01:16:39.880
I mean a person who's fond of you, too,

1281
01:16:39.960 --> 01:16:43.520
and likes you enough
to be interested in your character...

1282
01:16:44.920 --> 01:16:47.480
I think that's just as important
as college is.

1283
01:16:49.080 --> 01:16:50.960
And even more so.

1284
01:16:52.760 --> 01:16:54.280
That's what I think.

1285
01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:57.120
I think it's awfully important, too.

1286
01:16:57.880 --> 01:16:59.280
Emily?

1287
01:16:59.360 --> 01:17:01.520
Y... yes, George?

1288
01:17:01.600 --> 01:17:04.680
Emily, if I do improve
and make a big change,

1289
01:17:04.760 --> 01:17:07.640
do you think you...?
I mean, could you be...?

1290
01:17:07.720 --> 01:17:08.680
I...

1291
01:17:12.720 --> 01:17:14.320
I am now.

1292
01:17:18.400 --> 01:17:20.440
I always have been.

1293
01:17:22.600 --> 01:17:25.320
Oh, so I guess it's an important talk
we've been having.

1294
01:17:26.840 --> 01:17:29.800
[LAUGHING] Yes. Yes.

1295
01:17:29.880 --> 01:17:32.200
Wait just a minute and I'll walk you home.

1296
01:17:33.160 --> 01:17:34.520
Mr Morgan!

1297
01:17:38.600 --> 01:17:41.640
Er, I have to go home
and get the money to pay you for this.

1298
01:17:41.720 --> 01:17:45.480
– But it'll only take me a minute.
– What's that? George Gibbs!

1299
01:17:45.560 --> 01:17:51.000
– Do you mean to tell me...?
– Yes, but... I had reasons, Mr Morgan.

1300
01:17:51.080 --> 01:17:53.920
Look, here's my gold watch
to keep until I come back with the money.

1301
01:17:54.000 --> 01:17:56.000
It's all right. Keep your watch.

1302
01:17:56.080 --> 01:17:58.320
– I'll trust you.
– I'll be back in five minutes.

1303
01:17:58.400 --> 01:18:01.240
I'll trust you ten years, George!

1304
01:18:01.800 --> 01:18:03.480
Not a day over.

1305
01:18:04.680 --> 01:18:08.480
– Got over all your shock now, Emily?
– Yes, thank you, Mr Morgan.

1306
01:18:11.080 --> 01:18:12.680
It was nothing.

1307
01:18:18.440 --> 01:18:19.920
I'm ready.

1308
01:18:34.120 --> 01:18:36.320
Well...

1309
01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:39.680
Now we're ready
to get on with the wedding!

1310
01:18:39.760 --> 01:18:42.360
[♪ BRIGHT MELODY]

1311
01:19:23.480 --> 01:19:26.800
There are a lot of things
to be said about a wedding.

1312
01:19:27.560 --> 01:19:32.080
There are a lot of thoughts
that go on during a wedding.

1313
01:19:32.160 --> 01:19:34.280
We can't get them all
into one wedding, naturally,

1314
01:19:34.360 --> 01:19:38.120
especially not a wedding at Grover's
Corners, where they're awfully plain

1315
01:19:38.200 --> 01:19:39.800
and short.

1316
01:19:39.880 --> 01:19:43.040
In this wedding, I play the minister.

1317
01:19:43.800 --> 01:19:47.080
And that gives me the right to say
a few more things about it.

1318
01:19:47.160 --> 01:19:50.000
For a while now,
the play gets pretty serious.

1319
01:19:50.080 --> 01:19:55.000
You see, some churches say
that marriage is a sacrament.

1320
01:19:56.760 --> 01:19:59.440
I don't quite know what that means,
but I can guess.

1321
01:19:59.520 --> 01:20:02.040
It's like Mrs Gibbs said
a few minutes ago,

1322
01:20:02.120 --> 01:20:06.040
people were made to live two-by-two.

1323
01:20:07.680 --> 01:20:10.600
Now, this is a good wedding,
but people are so put together

1324
01:20:10.680 --> 01:20:14.400
that even at a good wedding
there's a lot of confusion

1325
01:20:14.480 --> 01:20:17.360
way down deep in people's minds

1326
01:20:17.440 --> 01:20:19.840
and we thought
that ought to be in our play, too.

1327
01:20:20.720 --> 01:20:23.960
The real hero of this scene
isn't on the stage at all.

1328
01:20:25.280 --> 01:20:27.400
And you know who that is.

1329
01:20:27.480 --> 01:20:31.840
It's like what, um...
oh, one of those European fellas said –

1330
01:20:31.920 --> 01:20:35.200
every child born into the world

1331
01:20:35.280 --> 01:20:40.920
is nature's attempt
to make a perfect human being.

1332
01:20:42.600 --> 01:20:45.880
Well, we've seen nature pushing
and contriving for some time.

1333
01:20:46.800 --> 01:20:49.600
We all know
nature's interested in quantity.

1334
01:20:50.880 --> 01:20:54.120
But I think she's interested
in quality, too.

1335
01:20:54.200 --> 01:20:56.240
Well, that's why I'm in the ministry.

1336
01:20:57.400 --> 01:21:01.040
And don't forget all the other witnesses
at this wedding –

1337
01:21:01.120 --> 01:21:02.800
the ancestors.

1338
01:21:03.680 --> 01:21:07.480
Millions of them. Most of them set out
to live two-by-two, also.

1339
01:21:08.680 --> 01:21:11.360
Millions of them.

1340
01:21:12.960 --> 01:21:15.080
Well, that's all my sermon.

1341
01:21:15.160 --> 01:21:16.760
'Twan't very long, anyway.

1342
01:21:16.840 --> 01:21:19.120
[CHURCH BELL RINGS]

1343
01:21:29.400 --> 01:21:32.240
MRS WEBB: I don't know why on Earth
I should be crying.

1344
01:21:33.200 --> 01:21:35.720
I suppose there's nothing to cry about.

1345
01:21:37.440 --> 01:21:40.600
It came over me this morning at breakfast.

1346
01:21:40.680 --> 01:21:46.680
There was Emily eating her breakfast,
as she's done for 17 years.

1347
01:21:48.280 --> 01:21:51.680
And now she's going off to eat it
at somebody else's house.

1348
01:21:53.200 --> 01:21:55.080
I suppose that's it.

1349
01:21:56.040 --> 01:21:58.120
And then Emily,

1350
01:21:58.200 --> 01:22:00.600
she suddenly said....

1351
01:22:00.680 --> 01:22:03.320
'I can't eat another mouthful.'

1352
01:22:04.600 --> 01:22:08.080
And she put her head down
on the table and she cried.

1353
01:22:10.400 --> 01:22:12.240
Oh, I've got to say it.

1354
01:22:13.600 --> 01:22:15.600
You know, there's something
downright-cruel

1355
01:22:15.680 --> 01:22:19.280
about sending our girls out
into marriage this way.

1356
01:22:21.680 --> 01:22:27.040
I hope her girlfriends
have told her a thing or two.

1357
01:22:28.720 --> 01:22:34.400
It's cruel, I know, but I couldn't
bring myself to say anything.

1358
01:22:35.920 --> 01:22:38.920
I went into it as blind as a bat myself.

1359
01:22:41.760 --> 01:22:44.000
The whole world's wrong.

1360
01:22:45.720 --> 01:22:47.680
That's what's the matter.

1361
01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:50.000
[♪ ORGAN BEGINS SOFTLY]

1362
01:22:50.080 --> 01:22:51.920
There they come.

1363
01:22:52.000 --> 01:22:53.680
[JEERING] George! Hast!

1364
01:22:53.760 --> 01:22:55.200
[WHOOPING]

1365
01:22:55.280 --> 01:22:58.440
Look at him, fellas!
Ooh, he looks scared to death.

1366
01:22:58.520 --> 01:23:00.000
[LAUGHTER]

1367
01:23:00.080 --> 01:23:02.240
George, don't look so innocent,
you old geezer.

1368
01:23:02.320 --> 01:23:06.120
– We know what you're thinking, boy!
– Don't disgrace the team, big boy.

1369
01:23:06.200 --> 01:23:09.120
All right, that'll do!
That's enough of that.

1370
01:23:09.200 --> 01:23:13.080
There used to be an awful lot of that kind
of thing at weddings in the old days.

1371
01:23:13.160 --> 01:23:15.000
Rome, and later.

1372
01:23:15.080 --> 01:23:18.560
We're more civilised now – or so they say.

1373
01:23:18.640 --> 01:23:21.960
[♪ Piano plays introduction
to 'Love Divine, All Loves Excelling']

1374
01:23:25.120 --> 01:23:30.480
♪ Gariad dwyfol uwch bob cariad

1375
01:23:30.560 --> 01:23:35.920
♪ Londer nefoedd, tyrd i lawr... ♪

1376
01:23:37.120 --> 01:23:38.560
MRS GIBBS: George?

1377
01:23:38.640 --> 01:23:40.720
[SINGING CONTINUES]

1378
01:23:40.800 --> 01:23:44.880
– George, what's the matter?
– Mam, I don't want to grow old.

1379
01:23:44.960 --> 01:23:49.000
– Why is everybody pushing me so?
– Why, George, you wanted it.

1380
01:23:49.080 --> 01:23:50.800
– No, Mam, listen to me...
– No!

1381
01:23:50.880 --> 01:23:53.560
– No, George, you are a man now.
– No, Mam.

1382
01:23:53.640 --> 01:23:57.480
For the last time I ask you –
all I want to do is...

1383
01:23:57.560 --> 01:23:58.880
to be a fella.

1384
01:23:58.960 --> 01:24:02.400
George! If anyone should hear you!
Now stop.

1385
01:24:04.000 --> 01:24:06.080
Why, I am ashamed of you!

1386
01:24:08.080 --> 01:24:09.800
Oh, George.

1387
01:24:12.680 --> 01:24:14.000
What?

1388
01:24:16.360 --> 01:24:18.000
Where's Emily?

1389
01:24:18.720 --> 01:24:22.280
– Oh, George, you give me such a turn.
– Cheer up, Mam.

1390
01:24:23.520 --> 01:24:28.000
– I'm getting married.
– Just let me catch my breath a minute.

1391
01:24:28.080 --> 01:24:31.640
Mam! Now, you save Thursday nights.

1392
01:24:32.520 --> 01:24:36.200
Emily and I are coming over to dinner
every Thursday night, you'll see.

1393
01:24:38.600 --> 01:24:40.760
Mam, what are you crying for?

1394
01:24:40.840 --> 01:24:43.240
Come on, we've got to get ready for this.

1395
01:24:43.960 --> 01:24:46.840
[♪ Piano plays introduction
to 'Blessed Be The Tie That Binds']

1396
01:24:48.760 --> 01:24:53.840
♪ Mae Un sy'n ein tynnu ynghyd

1397
01:24:53.920 --> 01:24:58.560
♪ I brofi holl gariad y Nef

1398
01:24:58.640 --> 01:25:10.520
♪ Mewn glân gymdeithas unwn i gyd
i foli ei enw Ef ♪

1399
01:25:11.640 --> 01:25:15.160
EMILY: I never felt so alone
in my whole life.

1400
01:25:15.720 --> 01:25:18.480
And George over there, looking so...!

1401
01:25:19.600 --> 01:25:21.600
[RAPID, SHALLOW BREATHING]

1402
01:25:21.680 --> 01:25:23.680
I hate him!

1403
01:25:23.760 --> 01:25:25.680
I wish I were dead.

1404
01:25:26.720 --> 01:25:27.880
Dad?

1405
01:25:28.840 --> 01:25:30.560
– Dad!
– Emily?

1406
01:25:31.320 --> 01:25:35.120
– Emily, now don't get upset.
– But, Dad, I don't wanna get married.

1407
01:25:35.200 --> 01:25:36.880
Ssh, Emily, everything is all right.

1408
01:25:36.960 --> 01:25:40.080
Why can't I stay for a while just as I am?

1409
01:25:40.160 --> 01:25:42.800
– Let's go away.
– No, no.

1410
01:25:42.880 --> 01:25:44.320
Now, stop and think a minute.

1411
01:25:44.400 --> 01:25:46.440
Don't you remember
that you used to say...?

1412
01:25:46.520 --> 01:25:50.480
All the time, you used to say –
all the time – that I was your girl.

1413
01:25:51.520 --> 01:25:55.600
There must be lots of places we can go to.
I'll work for you. I could keep house.

1414
01:25:55.680 --> 01:25:59.920
Ssh! You shouldn't think of such things.
You are just nervous, Emily.

1415
01:26:03.560 --> 01:26:04.680
George.

1416
01:26:06.640 --> 01:26:07.640
George!

1417
01:26:09.920 --> 01:26:12.480
Will you come here a minute?
[NERVOUS CHUCKLE]

1418
01:26:13.040 --> 01:26:17.440
Why, you are marrying
the finest young fellow in the world.

1419
01:26:17.520 --> 01:26:20.600
– George is a fine fellow.
– But, Dad...

1420
01:26:22.600 --> 01:26:25.120
I'm giving away my daughter, George.

1421
01:26:25.880 --> 01:26:29.200
– Do you think you can take care of her?
– Mr Webb, I want to...

1422
01:26:31.040 --> 01:26:32.120
I want to try.

1423
01:26:33.640 --> 01:26:35.680
Emily, I'm gonna do my best.

1424
01:26:37.920 --> 01:26:39.600
I love you, Emily.

1425
01:26:40.200 --> 01:26:41.760
I need you.

1426
01:26:42.640 --> 01:26:45.520
Well, if you love me...

1427
01:26:46.960 --> 01:26:48.200
..help me.

1428
01:26:49.000 --> 01:26:51.480
All I want is someone to love me.

1429
01:26:51.560 --> 01:26:53.240
I will, Emily.

1430
01:26:54.520 --> 01:26:55.480
Emily, I'll try.

1431
01:26:55.560 --> 01:26:57.440
And I mean for ever.

1432
01:26:58.160 --> 01:26:59.520
Do you hear?

1433
01:27:01.080 --> 01:27:02.960
For ever and ever.

1434
01:27:07.240 --> 01:27:08.800
George?

1435
01:27:10.520 --> 01:27:14.400
– [♪ Piano plays Wagner's 'Bridal Chorus']
– Come, they're waiting for us.

1436
01:27:15.320 --> 01:27:17.400
Now, you know it'll be all right.

1437
01:27:17.480 --> 01:27:18.800
Come, quick.

1438
01:27:22.800 --> 01:27:24.800
Do you, George,

1439
01:27:24.880 --> 01:27:30.280
take this woman, Emily,
to be your wedded wife, to have...

1440
01:27:30.360 --> 01:27:33.400
Ah! Perfectly lovely wedding!

1441
01:27:34.000 --> 01:27:36.240
Loveliest wedding I ever saw.

1442
01:27:36.320 --> 01:27:39.000
I do love a good wedding, don't you?

1443
01:27:39.080 --> 01:27:42.840
Ah, doesn't she make a lovely bride?

1444
01:27:44.120 --> 01:27:45.360
I do.

1445
01:27:45.440 --> 01:27:49.120
Do you, Emily, take this man, George,

1446
01:27:49.200 --> 01:27:50.800
to be your wedded husband...?

1447
01:27:50.880 --> 01:27:53.480
Don't know when I've seen
such a lovely wedding!

1448
01:27:54.640 --> 01:27:56.640
I always cry. [GIGGLES]

1449
01:27:56.720 --> 01:27:59.120
I don't know why that is,
but I always cry.

1450
01:27:59.880 --> 01:28:03.880
I just like to see young people happy,
don't you?

1451
01:28:03.960 --> 01:28:06.520
Oh, I think it's lovely.

1452
01:28:13.120 --> 01:28:16.120
[GEORGE'S FRIENDS CHEER]

1453
01:28:16.200 --> 01:28:18.000
[ALL STOP]

1454
01:28:29.240 --> 01:28:32.880
I've married over 200 couples in my day.

1455
01:28:35.000 --> 01:28:36.720
Do I believe in it?

1456
01:28:39.800 --> 01:28:41.640
I don't know.

1457
01:28:41.720 --> 01:28:43.480
M...

1458
01:28:43.560 --> 01:28:45.680
marries N.

1459
01:28:47.360 --> 01:28:49.160
Millions of 'em.

1460
01:28:49.240 --> 01:28:53.280
The cottage, the go-cart,
the Sunday afternoon drives in the Ford,

1461
01:28:53.360 --> 01:28:55.200
the first rheumatism,

1462
01:28:55.280 --> 01:28:56.960
the grandchildren,

1463
01:28:57.040 --> 01:28:59.440
the second rheumatism,

1464
01:28:59.520 --> 01:29:01.200
the deathbed.

1465
01:29:02.080 --> 01:29:03.960
The reading of the will.

1466
01:29:09.000 --> 01:29:12.280
Once in 1,000 times...

1467
01:29:13.880 --> 01:29:15.680
..it's interesting.

1468
01:29:18.000 --> 01:29:20.560
Well, let's have Mendelssohn's
Wedding March!

1469
01:29:20.640 --> 01:29:23.440
[♪ Piano plays Mendelssohn's
'Wedding March']

1470
01:29:23.520 --> 01:29:25.600
[CHEERS, WHOOPS]

1471
01:29:26.880 --> 01:29:30.480
MRS SOAMES: Oh, aren't they
a lovely couple?

1472
01:29:31.280 --> 01:29:33.520
Oh, I've never been
to such a nice wedding.

1473
01:29:34.600 --> 01:29:36.240
I'm sure they'll be happy.

1474
01:29:37.480 --> 01:29:39.800
I always say,

1475
01:29:39.880 --> 01:29:42.880
'Happiness, that is the great thing!'

1476
01:29:45.240 --> 01:29:47.520
[VOICE BREAKING] The important thing...

1477
01:29:48.720 --> 01:29:50.520
..is to be happy.

1478
01:29:51.320 --> 01:29:53.480
[♪ UP-TEMPO MELODY]

1479
01:29:54.400 --> 01:29:56.640
[LAUGHTER, CHATTER]

1480
01:30:52.320 --> 01:30:54.040
♪ Hi! Hi! Hi!
Whoa!

1481
01:30:54.120 --> 01:30:55.720
♪ Hi! Hi! Hi!
Whoa!

1482
01:30:55.800 --> 01:30:57.560
♪ Hi! Hi! Hi!
Whoa! ♪

1483
01:31:28.800 --> 01:31:31.600
[MUSIC DROWNS OUT SPEECH]

1484
01:31:47.280 --> 01:31:48.920
[♪ STOPS]

1485
01:31:54.600 --> 01:31:57.240
STAGE MANAGER:
That's all the first half, folks.

1486
01:31:57.320 --> 01:31:59.840
20 minutes' intermission.

1487
01:32:02.440 --> 01:32:04.480
(APPLAUSE, CHEERS)

1488
01:33:28.440 --> 01:33:31.040
[♪ PIANO PLAYS GENTLE MELANCHOLIC MELODY]

1489
01:33:52.360 --> 01:33:54.240
[THEY LAUGH]

1490
01:33:58.920 --> 01:34:01.160
[♪ MELODY SWELLS]

1491
01:34:55.280 --> 01:34:59.280
This time,
nine years have gone by, friends.

1492
01:35:00.800 --> 01:35:02.240
Summer.

1493
01:35:03.560 --> 01:35:05.280
1913.

1494
01:35:12.880 --> 01:35:16.760
Gradual changes in Grover's Corners.

1495
01:35:16.840 --> 01:35:18.760
[STRAINS]

1496
01:35:18.840 --> 01:35:20.680
Horses are getting rarer.

1497
01:35:22.080 --> 01:35:25.120
Farmers coming into town in Fords.

1498
01:35:25.200 --> 01:35:26.640
[CHUCKLES]

1499
01:35:28.880 --> 01:35:32.000
Everybody locks their house doors
now at night.

1500
01:35:33.760 --> 01:35:36.640
Haven't been any burglars in town, yet.

1501
01:35:37.560 --> 01:35:39.880
But everybody's heard about 'em.

1502
01:35:39.960 --> 01:35:41.840
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

1503
01:35:42.720 --> 01:35:44.480
You'd be surprised, though.

1504
01:35:45.520 --> 01:35:48.880
On the whole,
things don't change much round here.

1505
01:35:51.600 --> 01:35:55.000
This is certainly an important part
of Grover's Corners.

1506
01:35:56.320 --> 01:35:58.240
It's on a hilltop.

1507
01:35:58.320 --> 01:36:00.880
A windy hilltop.

1508
01:36:02.520 --> 01:36:04.920
Lots of sky, lots of clouds.

1509
01:36:06.040 --> 01:36:10.440
Often lots of sun and moon and stars.

1510
01:36:10.520 --> 01:36:15.360
You come up here on a fine afternoon,
oh, you can see range on range of hills.

1511
01:36:16.000 --> 01:36:20.040
Awful blue they are – up there
by Llyn y Gwyddau and Llyn yr Ysbryd.

1512
01:36:20.120 --> 01:36:22.120
And way up, if you've got a glass,

1513
01:36:22.200 --> 01:36:24.880
you can see the Mynyddoedd Gwynion

1514
01:36:24.960 --> 01:36:27.760
and Mynydd Gwesa,
where Penconwy and Conwy is.

1515
01:36:28.680 --> 01:36:33.160
And, of course, our favourite mountain,
Mynydd Unig, is right here.

1516
01:36:34.560 --> 01:36:36.720
And all these towns that lie around it.

1517
01:36:38.080 --> 01:36:39.880
Treiffre.

1518
01:36:39.960 --> 01:36:42.000
N' Dwyrain Iffre.

1519
01:36:42.080 --> 01:36:43.360
N' Trepedr.

1520
01:36:44.800 --> 01:36:46.800
N' Dulyn.

1521
01:36:46.880 --> 01:36:49.040
And there,

1522
01:36:49.120 --> 01:36:52.440
quite a way down,
is Grover's Corners.

1523
01:36:55.160 --> 01:36:59.600
Yes. Oh, beautiful spot up here.

1524
01:36:59.680 --> 01:37:03.840
[SNIFFS DEEPLY]
Oh, mountain laurel, lilacs.

1525
01:37:04.440 --> 01:37:08.120
I often wonder why people like
to be buried in Dôl y Coed and Gorsdir

1526
01:37:08.200 --> 01:37:12.120
when they might pass the same time
up here in New Hampshire.

1527
01:37:15.680 --> 01:37:17.560
Over there are the old stones.

1528
01:37:18.400 --> 01:37:20.600
1670, 1680.

1529
01:37:22.200 --> 01:37:26.280
Strong-minded people
that come a long way to be independent.

1530
01:37:27.360 --> 01:37:33.680
Summer people walk around there laughing
'at the funny words on the tombstones'.

1531
01:37:35.880 --> 01:37:37.720
It don't do any harm.

1532
01:37:37.800 --> 01:37:40.560
And genealogists come up from Boston,

1533
01:37:40.640 --> 01:37:45.320
get paid – well, by city people –
for looking up their ancestors.

1534
01:37:45.400 --> 01:37:47.480
[LAUGHS]

1535
01:37:48.680 --> 01:37:51.280
I guess it don't do no harm, either.

1536
01:37:51.360 --> 01:37:54.960
Wherever you come near the human race,
there's layers and layers of nonsense.

1537
01:37:56.640 --> 01:38:01.720
Now, this here is the new part
of the cemetery.

1538
01:38:03.920 --> 01:38:05.720
Here's your friend, Mrs Gibbs.

1539
01:38:06.800 --> 01:38:08.880
And let me see...

1540
01:38:08.960 --> 01:38:14.000
Here's Mr Stimson,
the organist at the Congregational Church.

1541
01:38:14.080 --> 01:38:18.440
And Mrs Soames,
who enjoyed the wedding so, you remember?

1542
01:38:20.240 --> 01:38:22.120
Oh, and a lot of others.

1543
01:38:24.280 --> 01:38:26.840
And Editor Webb's boy, Wallace.

1544
01:38:28.040 --> 01:38:32.280
Whose appendix burst while he was
on a Boy Scout trip to Crawford Notch.

1545
01:38:34.520 --> 01:38:41.520
Yes, an awful lot of sorrow
has sort of quietened down up here.

1546
01:38:43.200 --> 01:38:47.440
People just wild with grief have brought
their relatives up to this hill.

1547
01:38:49.600 --> 01:38:51.200
We all know how it is.

1548
01:38:53.400 --> 01:38:55.440
And then time...

1549
01:38:56.640 --> 01:38:59.240
..and sunny days.

1550
01:38:59.320 --> 01:39:00.840
and rainy days...

1551
01:39:02.520 --> 01:39:04.000
..and snow.

1552
01:39:04.080 --> 01:39:06.080
We're just glad
they're in a beautiful place

1553
01:39:06.160 --> 01:39:08.920
and we're coming up here ourselves,
when our fit's over.

1554
01:39:09.000 --> 01:39:11.440
Now, there are some things we all know,

1555
01:39:11.520 --> 01:39:14.120
but we don't take 'em out
and look at 'em very often.

1556
01:39:14.200 --> 01:39:18.280
We all know that something is eternal.

1557
01:39:20.200 --> 01:39:22.280
And it ain't houses and it ain't names,

1558
01:39:22.360 --> 01:39:24.960
and it ain't earth
and it ain't even the stars.

1559
01:39:25.040 --> 01:39:28.960
Everybody knows in their bones
that something is eternal,

1560
01:39:29.040 --> 01:39:31.080
and that something has to do
with human beings.

1561
01:39:31.160 --> 01:39:35.640
All the greatest people ever lived
have been telling us that for 5,000 years,

1562
01:39:35.720 --> 01:39:40.040
and yet you'd be surprised
how people are always losing hold of it.

1563
01:39:41.360 --> 01:39:47.600
There is something way down deep
that's eternal about every human being.

1564
01:39:51.320 --> 01:39:53.160
Now, you know as well as I do

1565
01:39:53.240 --> 01:39:57.080
that the dead don't stay interested
in us living people for very long.

1566
01:39:58.280 --> 01:40:00.840
Gradually...

1567
01:40:00.920 --> 01:40:04.320
gradually, they lose hold of the Earth

1568
01:40:04.400 --> 01:40:07.040
and the ambitions they had

1569
01:40:07.120 --> 01:40:09.400
and the pleasures they had.

1570
01:40:10.640 --> 01:40:12.720
And the things they suffered.

1571
01:40:13.840 --> 01:40:17.040
And the people they loved.
They get weaned away from Earth.

1572
01:40:18.040 --> 01:40:20.440
That's the way I put it, 'weaned away'.

1573
01:40:22.400 --> 01:40:29.120
And they stay here while the Earth part
of 'em burns away, burns out.

1574
01:40:30.760 --> 01:40:34.200
And all that time,
they slowly get indifferent

1575
01:40:34.280 --> 01:40:36.840
to what's going on in Grover's Corners.

1576
01:40:40.480 --> 01:40:42.200
They're waiting.

1577
01:40:44.000 --> 01:40:46.960
They're waiting for something
that they feel is coming.

1578
01:40:47.040 --> 01:40:50.040
Something important, and great.

1579
01:40:50.120 --> 01:40:53.480
Aren't they waiting for the eternal part
in them to come out?

1580
01:40:55.720 --> 01:40:57.120
Clear.

1581
01:40:59.040 --> 01:41:02.640
Now, some of the things they're gonna say
maybe'll hurt your feelings,

1582
01:41:02.720 --> 01:41:04.440
but that's the way it is.

1583
01:41:04.520 --> 01:41:06.920
Mother and daughter, husband and wife,

1584
01:41:07.000 --> 01:41:08.960
enemy and enemy, mother and miser,

1585
01:41:09.040 --> 01:41:12.640
all those terribly important things
kind of grow pale round here.

1586
01:41:15.960 --> 01:41:20.520
What's left when memory's gone?

1587
01:41:23.040 --> 01:41:24.520
And your identity.

1588
01:41:27.400 --> 01:41:29.160
Mrs Smith?

1589
01:41:31.080 --> 01:41:33.640
Well, there are some living people.

1590
01:41:33.720 --> 01:41:39.440
There's Joe Stoddard, our undertaker,
supervising a new-made grave.

1591
01:41:40.680 --> 01:41:44.200
And here comes a Grover's Corners boy
that left town to go out West.

1592
01:41:47.160 --> 01:41:49.120
MAN: Good afternoon, Joe Stoddard.

1593
01:41:49.200 --> 01:41:52.040
Oh, good afternoon, good afternoon.

1594
01:41:53.080 --> 01:41:56.200
Let me see now, do I know you?

1595
01:41:56.280 --> 01:42:00.640
– I'm Sam Craig.
– Gracious sakes' alive! Of all people!

1596
01:42:00.720 --> 01:42:03.560
I should have knowed
you'd be back for the funeral.

1597
01:42:03.640 --> 01:42:07.560
– You've been away a long time, Sam.
– Yeah, I've been away over 12 years.

1598
01:42:07.640 --> 01:42:09.880
– You look well.
– Yes, yes.

1599
01:42:09.960 --> 01:42:12.360
Can't complain.

1600
01:42:12.440 --> 01:42:15.080
Very sad, our journey today, Samuel.

1601
01:42:16.360 --> 01:42:18.120
– Yes.
– Yes, yes.

1602
01:42:18.200 --> 01:42:22.560
I always say I hate to supervise
when a young person is taken.

1603
01:42:22.640 --> 01:42:25.360
They'll be here in a few minutes now.

1604
01:42:25.440 --> 01:42:27.840
I had to come here early today.

1605
01:42:27.920 --> 01:42:30.360
My son is supervising at the home.

1606
01:42:32.120 --> 01:42:34.120
SAM: Old Farmer McCarty.

1607
01:42:34.200 --> 01:42:37.160
I used to do chores for him after school.
He had the lumbago.

1608
01:42:37.240 --> 01:42:40.640
Yes, we brought Farmer McCarty here
a number of years ago now.

1609
01:42:40.720 --> 01:42:43.160
Why, it's my Aunt Julia!

1610
01:42:43.240 --> 01:42:45.160
I'd forgotten she'd...

1611
01:42:47.640 --> 01:42:49.200
Of course.

1612
01:42:50.280 --> 01:42:51.200
Of course.

1613
01:42:51.280 --> 01:42:55.720
Yes, Doc Gibbs lost his wife,
oh, two, three years ago.

1614
01:42:55.800 --> 01:42:57.680
About this time.

1615
01:42:57.760 --> 01:43:00.680
And today's another
pretty bad blow for him, too.

1616
01:43:02.240 --> 01:43:07.080
MRS GIBBS: That's my sister Carey's boy,
Sam. Sam... Craig.

1617
01:43:07.160 --> 01:43:09.480
SIMON: I'm always uncomfortable
when they're around.

1618
01:43:09.560 --> 01:43:12.320
MRS GIBBS: Simon!
SAM: Do they choose their own verses much?

1619
01:43:12.400 --> 01:43:15.880
No, not usual.
Mostly the bereaved pick a verse.

1620
01:43:15.960 --> 01:43:18.520
Doesn't sound like Aunt Julia.

1621
01:43:18.600 --> 01:43:21.440
There aren't many
of those Hersey sisters left now.

1622
01:43:22.160 --> 01:43:25.000
Let's see.... I'm looking
for my father and my mother's...

1623
01:43:25.080 --> 01:43:28.000
Over there with the Craigs.
Avenue F.

1624
01:43:33.440 --> 01:43:35.160
He was organist at church, wasn't he?

1625
01:43:36.440 --> 01:43:38.800
Drank a lot, we used to say.

1626
01:43:38.880 --> 01:43:43.360
Nobody was supposed to know about it.
He'd seen a peck of trouble.

1627
01:43:44.680 --> 01:43:46.520
Took his own life, you know?

1628
01:43:47.440 --> 01:43:49.520
– Oh, did he?
– Yeah.

1629
01:43:49.600 --> 01:43:51.680
Hung himself in the attic.

1630
01:43:51.760 --> 01:43:55.200
They tried to hush it up,
but of course it got around.

1631
01:43:55.280 --> 01:44:00.680
He chose his own epitaph. Go on, you can
see it there. It isn't a verse, exactly.

1632
01:44:02.040 --> 01:44:04.080
Why, it's just some notes of music.

1633
01:44:05.200 --> 01:44:07.240
– What is it?
– Oh, I wouldn't know.

1634
01:44:07.320 --> 01:44:10.360
It was wrote up
in the Boston papers at the time.

1635
01:44:12.280 --> 01:44:14.440
What did she die of, Joe?

1636
01:44:14.520 --> 01:44:16.080
– Who?
– My cousin.

1637
01:44:16.160 --> 01:44:18.160
Oh, didn't you know?

1638
01:44:18.240 --> 01:44:21.360
Had some trouble
bringing a baby into the world.

1639
01:44:21.440 --> 01:44:23.920
Yeah, it was her second as well.

1640
01:44:24.000 --> 01:44:27.400
There's a little boy,
he's about four years old.

1641
01:44:28.960 --> 01:44:31.040
And the grave's going to be by here?

1642
01:44:31.120 --> 01:44:34.440
Well, there ain't much more room
over there among the Gibbses,

1643
01:44:34.520 --> 01:44:39.440
so they're opening up a whole new
Gibbs section over here, by Avenue B.

1644
01:44:40.680 --> 01:44:44.640
Oh, you'll excuse me now.
I see they're coming.

1645
01:45:22.240 --> 01:45:24.160
MRS SOAMES: Who is it, Julia?

1646
01:45:24.240 --> 01:45:29.040
– My daughter-in-law, Emily Webb.
– Well, I declare.

1647
01:45:29.960 --> 01:45:31.920
The road up here must have been
awful muddy.

1648
01:45:32.000 --> 01:45:34.920
– What did she die of, Julia?
– In childbirth.

1649
01:45:35.640 --> 01:45:37.520
MRS SOAMES: Childbirth?

1650
01:45:38.920 --> 01:45:42.200
Oh, I'd forgotten all about that.

1651
01:45:43.480 --> 01:45:46.840
My, wasn't life awful?

1652
01:45:49.400 --> 01:45:51.000
And wonderful.

1653
01:45:51.080 --> 01:45:54.960
– SIMON: Wonderful what it...?
– MRS GIBBS: Simon! Now, remember!

1654
01:45:55.040 --> 01:45:57.120
I remember Emily's wedding.

1655
01:45:58.160 --> 01:46:00.320
Wasn't it a lovely wedding?

1656
01:46:01.880 --> 01:46:06.120
And I remember her reading the class poem
at graduation exercises.

1657
01:46:06.200 --> 01:46:09.160
Emily was the brightest girl
ever graduated high school.

1658
01:46:09.240 --> 01:46:12.680
I've heard Principal Wilkins say so
time after time.

1659
01:46:14.760 --> 01:46:17.840
I called in on them at their new farm,
just before I died.

1660
01:46:19.000 --> 01:46:20.560
A perfectly beautiful farm.

1661
01:46:20.640 --> 01:46:22.520
DEAD MAN: It's on the same road
we lived on.

1662
01:46:22.600 --> 01:46:25.120
DEAD WOMAN: Yeah, right smart farm.

1663
01:46:42.840 --> 01:46:47.920
♪ Pan fyddwn ar daith ar wahân... ♪

1664
01:46:48.000 --> 01:46:50.440
MRS SOAMES: Ah, I always liked that hymn.

1665
01:46:51.400 --> 01:46:53.840
I was hoping they'd sing a hymn.

1666
01:46:55.280 --> 01:47:03.720
♪ Cawn gwrdd yn yr enaid,
a chwrdd yn y gân

1667
01:47:03.800 --> 01:47:10.800
♪ Yn gyfan ryw ddydd a ddaw ♪

1668
01:47:12.600 --> 01:47:14.240
EMILY: Hello?

1669
01:47:14.320 --> 01:47:17.080
– MRS SOAMES: Hello, Emily.
– DEAD MAN: Hello, Mrs Gibbs.

1670
01:47:18.560 --> 01:47:20.280
EMILY: Hello, Mother Gibbs.

1671
01:47:20.360 --> 01:47:22.200
– MRS GIBBS: Emily.
– Hello!

1672
01:47:23.200 --> 01:47:25.400
– It's raining.
– Yes.

1673
01:47:26.000 --> 01:47:29.640
They'll be gone soon, dear.
Just rest yourself.

1674
01:47:29.720 --> 01:47:33.720
It seems thousands
and thousands of years since I...

1675
01:47:35.520 --> 01:47:38.920
Dad remembered that
that was my favourite hymn.

1676
01:47:54.760 --> 01:47:58.280
Oh, I wish I'd been here a long time.
I don't like being new here.

1677
01:48:00.200 --> 01:48:02.040
How do you do, Mr Stimson?

1678
01:48:02.600 --> 01:48:05.000
SIMON: How do you do, Emily.

1679
01:48:07.280 --> 01:48:09.440
EMILY: Mother Gibbs,

1680
01:48:09.520 --> 01:48:13.720
George and I have turned that farm
into just the best place you ever saw.

1681
01:48:13.800 --> 01:48:15.840
We thought of you all the time.

1682
01:48:15.920 --> 01:48:17.840
We wanted to show you the new barn

1683
01:48:17.920 --> 01:48:21.600
and a great long cement drinking fountain
for the stock.

1684
01:48:21.680 --> 01:48:24.120
We bought that
out of the money you left us.

1685
01:48:25.280 --> 01:48:27.480
MRS GIBBS: I did?

1686
01:48:27.560 --> 01:48:31.320
EMILY: Don't you remember, Mother Gibbs,
the legacy you left us?

1687
01:48:31.400 --> 01:48:33.840
Why, it was over $350.

1688
01:48:35.480 --> 01:48:39.400
MRS GIBBS: Yes... Yes, Emily.

1689
01:48:39.480 --> 01:48:43.480
EMILY: Well, there's a patent device
on the drinking fountain

1690
01:48:43.560 --> 01:48:46.200
so that it never overflows, Mother Gibbs,

1691
01:48:46.280 --> 01:48:50.320
and it never sinks below
a certain mark they have there.

1692
01:48:50.400 --> 01:48:52.280
Oh, it's fine.

1693
01:48:54.320 --> 01:48:58.360
It won't be the same to George without me,
but it's a lovely farm.

1694
01:49:02.040 --> 01:49:07.760
– Live people don't understand, do they?
– No, dear. Not very much.

1695
01:49:07.840 --> 01:49:11.960
They're sort of shut up in little boxes,
aren't they?

1696
01:49:12.040 --> 01:49:15.680
I feel as though I knew them last
1,000 years ago.

1697
01:49:18.800 --> 01:49:21.640
My boy is spending the day
at Mrs Carter's.

1698
01:49:23.080 --> 01:49:27.440
Oh, Mr Carter, my little boy
is spending the day at your house.

1699
01:49:27.520 --> 01:49:32.520
– Is he?
– Yes. Oh, he loves it there.

1700
01:49:32.600 --> 01:49:38.040
Oh, and Mother Gibbs, we have a Ford, too.

1701
01:49:38.120 --> 01:49:40.320
Never gives any trouble.

1702
01:49:41.480 --> 01:49:43.480
I don't drive, though.

1703
01:49:48.480 --> 01:49:50.400
Mother Gibbs,

1704
01:49:50.480 --> 01:49:55.200
when does this feeling go away,
of being one of them? How long...?

1705
01:49:55.280 --> 01:49:58.120
MRS GIBBS: Ssh, dear, just wait.

1706
01:49:59.520 --> 01:50:02.240
– Be patient.
– I know.

1707
01:50:03.880 --> 01:50:07.800
– Look, they're finished. They're going.
– Ssh.

1708
01:50:14.440 --> 01:50:16.000
EMILY: Look!

1709
01:50:16.080 --> 01:50:19.200
Father Gibbs is bringing
some of my flowers to you.

1710
01:50:19.280 --> 01:50:22.360
He looks just like George, doesn't he?

1711
01:50:27.560 --> 01:50:28.920
Oh, Mother Gibbs.

1712
01:50:30.520 --> 01:50:34.080
I never realised
before how troubled and how...

1713
01:50:35.680 --> 01:50:38.000
..how in the dark live persons are.

1714
01:50:38.720 --> 01:50:40.120
Look at him!

1715
01:50:42.120 --> 01:50:44.120
Oh, I loved him so.

1716
01:50:45.600 --> 01:50:48.640
From morning till night,
that's all they are.

1717
01:50:49.840 --> 01:50:51.360
Is troubled.

1718
01:50:52.680 --> 01:50:54.920
DEAD WOMAN 1: Little cooler than it was.

1719
01:50:55.000 --> 01:50:56.920
DEAD WOMAN 2:
Yes, that rain's cooled it off a little.

1720
01:50:57.000 --> 01:50:59.960
DEAD WOMAN 1: Those northeast winds
always do the same thing, don't they?

1721
01:51:00.040 --> 01:51:02.760
DEAD MAN: If it isn't a rain,
it's a three-day blow.

1722
01:51:02.840 --> 01:51:04.080
[LAUGHTER]

1723
01:51:04.160 --> 01:51:06.480
EMILY: But Mother Gibbs, one can go back.

1724
01:51:07.640 --> 01:51:10.160
One can go back there again, into living.

1725
01:51:11.200 --> 01:51:12.560
I feel it.

1726
01:51:13.560 --> 01:51:16.760
I know it. Why just then for a moment
I was thinking about...

1727
01:51:19.440 --> 01:51:21.480
About the farm.

1728
01:51:21.560 --> 01:51:27.000
And for a minute, I was there,
and my baby was on my lap as plain as day.

1729
01:51:27.080 --> 01:51:29.840
MRS GIBBS: Yes, of course you can.

1730
01:51:29.920 --> 01:51:33.080
EMILY: I can go back there and live
all those days over again... why not?

1731
01:51:33.160 --> 01:51:37.120
MRS GIBBS: All I can say is, Emily, don't.

1732
01:51:38.800 --> 01:51:40.840
But it's true, isn't it?

1733
01:51:42.120 --> 01:51:44.600
I can go and live back there again.

1734
01:51:45.800 --> 01:51:47.040
Yes.

1735
01:51:47.840 --> 01:51:51.960
Some have tried,
but they soon come back here.

1736
01:51:52.960 --> 01:51:54.400
MRS GIBBS: Don't do it, Emily.

1737
01:51:54.480 --> 01:51:57.680
MRS SOAMES: Emily, don't.
It's not what you think it'd be.

1738
01:51:57.760 --> 01:51:59.880
But I won't live over a sad day.

1739
01:51:59.960 --> 01:52:02.080
I'll choose a happy one.

1740
01:52:02.160 --> 01:52:05.400
I'll choose the day
I first knew that I loved George.

1741
01:52:07.160 --> 01:52:09.320
Why should that be painful?

1742
01:52:12.600 --> 01:52:17.240
You not only live it,
but you watch yourself living it.

1743
01:52:17.320 --> 01:52:18.920
EMILY: Yes?

1744
01:52:19.000 --> 01:52:22.960
And as you watch, you see the thing
that they, down there, never see.

1745
01:52:23.040 --> 01:52:25.080
You know the future.

1746
01:52:25.160 --> 01:52:27.600
You see what's gonna happen afterwards.

1747
01:52:27.680 --> 01:52:30.240
EMILY: But is that painful? Why?

1748
01:52:30.320 --> 01:52:34.040
MRS GIBBS: That isn't the only reason
why you shouldn't do it, Emily.

1749
01:52:35.360 --> 01:52:40.680
When you've been here longer, you'll see
that our life here is to forget all that,

1750
01:52:40.760 --> 01:52:46.000
and think only of what's ahead,
and be ready for what's ahead.

1751
01:52:48.040 --> 01:52:50.200
When you've been here longer,
you'll understand.

1752
01:52:50.280 --> 01:52:54.800
EMILY: But Mother Gibbs, how can
I ever forget that life? It's all I know.

1753
01:52:54.880 --> 01:52:57.360
– It's all I had.
– MRS SOAMES: Oh, Emily.

1754
01:52:57.440 --> 01:52:59.360
It isn't wise. Really, it isn't.

1755
01:52:59.440 --> 01:53:01.520
EMILY: It's a thing
I must know for myself.

1756
01:53:01.600 --> 01:53:04.120
– I'll choose a happy day, anyway.
– MRS GIBBS: No.

1757
01:53:06.560 --> 01:53:09.280
At least...

1758
01:53:09.360 --> 01:53:14.000
choose an unimportant day, choose
the least important day of your life.

1759
01:53:16.520 --> 01:53:18.520
It will be important enough.

1760
01:53:21.800 --> 01:53:24.160
EMILY: Then it can't be
since I was married.

1761
01:53:26.080 --> 01:53:28.400
Or since the baby was born.

1762
01:53:31.960 --> 01:53:35.880
I can choose a birthday at least, can't I?

1763
01:53:37.680 --> 01:53:39.800
I choose...

1764
01:53:39.880 --> 01:53:43.800
– my 12th birthday.
– All right, February 11th, 1899.

1765
01:53:45.400 --> 01:53:48.560
A Tuesday.
Do you want any special time of day?

1766
01:53:48.640 --> 01:53:51.200
– Oh, I want the whole day.
– We'll begin at dawn.

1767
01:53:52.080 --> 01:53:54.440
You remember it had been snowing
for several days,

1768
01:53:54.520 --> 01:53:57.560
but it had stopped the night before
and they'd begun clearing the roads.

1769
01:53:57.640 --> 01:53:58.760
The sun is coming up.

1770
01:54:00.880 --> 01:54:02.680
EMILY: There's Stryd Fawr.

1771
01:54:03.600 --> 01:54:06.800
Why, that's Mr Morgan's drugstore
before he changed it.

1772
01:54:07.800 --> 01:54:10.880
– And there's the livery stable.
– Yes, it's 1899.

1773
01:54:10.960 --> 01:54:12.840
This is 14 years ago.

1774
01:54:12.920 --> 01:54:16.240
Oh, that's the town
I knew as a little girl!

1775
01:54:17.160 --> 01:54:22.160
And, look, there's the old white fence
that used to be around our house.

1776
01:54:22.240 --> 01:54:23.400
[LAUGHS]

1777
01:54:23.960 --> 01:54:28.200
Oh, I'd forgotten that! Oh, I love it so!

1778
01:54:30.080 --> 01:54:31.440
– Are they inside?
– Yes.

1779
01:54:31.520 --> 01:54:34.160
Your mother'll be coming downstairs
in a minute to make breakfast.

1780
01:54:34.240 --> 01:54:35.200
Will she?

1781
01:54:35.280 --> 01:54:37.040
You remember your father had been gone
for several days?

1782
01:54:37.120 --> 01:54:39.440
– He came back on the early-morning train.
– No.

1783
01:54:39.520 --> 01:54:43.400
He'd been back to his college
to make a speech at Clinton.

1784
01:54:43.480 --> 01:54:46.480
– HOWIE: Whoa, Bessie! Bessie!
– EMILY: Look!

1785
01:54:46.560 --> 01:54:48.920
There's Howie Newsome.

1786
01:54:49.000 --> 01:54:50.720
There's our policeman.

1787
01:54:51.680 --> 01:54:53.320
But he's dead. He died.

1788
01:54:53.400 --> 01:54:55.200
– Morning, Bill.
– Morning, Howie.

1789
01:54:55.280 --> 01:54:57.960
– You're up early.
– Been rescuing a party.

1790
01:54:58.040 --> 01:55:00.600
Darn near froze to death,
down near Trebwyl there.

1791
01:55:00.680 --> 01:55:03.160
Got drunk and lay out in the snowdrifts.

1792
01:55:03.240 --> 01:55:05.600
Thought he was in bed when I shook him!
[LAUGHS]

1793
01:55:05.680 --> 01:55:08.680
– EMILY: Why, there's Joe Crowell.
– JOE: Good morning, Mr Warren.

1794
01:55:08.760 --> 01:55:10.520
– Morning.
– Morning, Howie.

1795
01:55:10.600 --> 01:55:12.680
MRS WEBB: Children!

1796
01:55:12.760 --> 01:55:15.880
Wally! Emily! Time to get up.

1797
01:55:15.960 --> 01:55:18.880
EMILY: Mam? I'm here!

1798
01:55:20.440 --> 01:55:25.360
Oh, how young Mam looks!
I didn't know Mam was ever that young.

1799
01:55:25.440 --> 01:55:30.000
MRS WEBB: You can come and dress by
the kitchen fire if you like, but hurry.

1800
01:55:33.680 --> 01:55:35.760
Good morning, Mr Newsome.

1801
01:55:35.840 --> 01:55:39.840
– Ooh, it's cold.
– It's ten below by my barn, Mrs Webb.

1802
01:55:39.920 --> 01:55:42.440
Think of it! Keep yourself wrapped up.

1803
01:55:42.520 --> 01:55:46.760
EMILY: [AS CHILD] Mam, I can't find
my blue hair ribbon anywhere!

1804
01:55:46.840 --> 01:55:50.080
MRS WEBB: Just open your eyes, dear,
that's all.

1805
01:55:50.160 --> 01:55:54.120
I laid it out for you special
on the dresser, there.

1806
01:55:54.200 --> 01:55:56.760
Ooh, if it were a snake it would bite you.

1807
01:55:56.840 --> 01:55:58.600
[LAUGHING] Yes! Yes.

1808
01:55:58.680 --> 01:56:01.400
– Good morning, Bill.
– Morning, Mr Webb.

1809
01:56:01.480 --> 01:56:03.600
– You're up early.
– Yes.

1810
01:56:03.680 --> 01:56:06.960
Just been back to my old college.
Been any trouble here?

1811
01:56:07.040 --> 01:56:10.720
Well, this morning I was called out
to rescue a Polish fella.

1812
01:56:10.800 --> 01:56:14.320
– Darn near froze to death he was.
– We must get it in the paper.

1813
01:56:14.400 --> 01:56:16.360
Oh, wasn't much.

1814
01:56:16.440 --> 01:56:18.240
EMILY: [WHISPERS] Dad.

1815
01:56:18.320 --> 01:56:21.840
– Good morning, Mother.
– How did it go, Charles?

1816
01:56:21.920 --> 01:56:24.200
Oh, fine, I guess.
I told 'em a few things.

1817
01:56:25.840 --> 01:56:27.640
– Everything all right here?
– Yes.

1818
01:56:27.720 --> 01:56:30.320
Can't think of anything
that's happened special.

1819
01:56:30.400 --> 01:56:34.960
Been right cold. Howie Newsome says
it's ten below over to his barn.

1820
01:56:35.040 --> 01:56:38.120
Yes, well, it's colder than that
at the college.

1821
01:56:38.200 --> 01:56:40.440
Students' ears are falling off.

1822
01:56:41.080 --> 01:56:42.840
It's not Christian.

1823
01:56:42.920 --> 01:56:46.000
– Paper have any mistakes in it?
– None that I noticed.

1824
01:56:46.080 --> 01:56:48.240
Coffee is ready when you want it.

1825
01:56:48.320 --> 01:56:51.080
Charles!
Don't forget, it's Emily's birthday.

1826
01:56:51.160 --> 01:56:54.160
– Did you remember to get her something?
– Yes.

1827
01:56:54.240 --> 01:56:56.800
[PLAYFULLY] I've got something here.

1828
01:56:57.400 --> 01:56:59.200
Where's my girl?

1829
01:56:59.280 --> 01:57:01.080
– No!
– Where's my birthday girl?

1830
01:57:01.160 --> 01:57:06.040
Don't interrupt her now, Charles.
You can see her at breakfast.

1831
01:57:06.120 --> 01:57:08.400
She's slow enough as it is.

1832
01:57:08.480 --> 01:57:11.600
Hurry up, children! It's seven o'clock.

1833
01:57:11.680 --> 01:57:13.880
Now, I don't want to call you again.

1834
01:57:13.960 --> 01:57:15.640
I can't bear it!

1835
01:57:16.680 --> 01:57:19.320
They're so young

1836
01:57:19.400 --> 01:57:21.160
and beautiful.

1837
01:57:21.960 --> 01:57:24.480
Why did they ever have to get old?

1838
01:57:25.960 --> 01:57:28.720
Oh, Mam, I'm here.

1839
01:57:29.520 --> 01:57:31.400
I'm grown-up.

1840
01:57:32.960 --> 01:57:35.960
I love you all – everything.

1841
01:57:37.120 --> 01:57:40.160
I can't look at everything hard enough.

1842
01:57:44.560 --> 01:57:46.400
[BIRDSONG]

1843
01:57:54.840 --> 01:57:57.480
– Good morning, Mam.
– Well, now, dear,

1844
01:57:57.560 --> 01:58:02.320
A very happy birthday to my girl,
and many happy returns.

1845
01:58:02.400 --> 01:58:06.160
There are some surprises
waiting for you on the kitchen table.

1846
01:58:06.240 --> 01:58:08.640
Oh, Mam, you shouldn't have!

1847
01:58:09.800 --> 01:58:11.040
I can't, I can't.

1848
01:58:11.120 --> 01:58:15.640
But birthday or no birthday, I want you
to eat your breakfast good and slow.

1849
01:58:15.720 --> 01:58:18.720
I want you to grow up
and be a good strong girl.

1850
01:58:18.800 --> 01:58:22.680
That in the blue paper is
from your Aunt Carrie.

1851
01:58:22.760 --> 01:58:27.160
And I reckon you can guess
who brought the postcard album.

1852
01:58:27.240 --> 01:58:29.960
It was on the doorstep
when I brought in the milk.

1853
01:58:30.040 --> 01:58:31.640
George Gibbs.

1854
01:58:31.720 --> 01:58:35.560
Must have come over in the cold
pretty early. Right nice of him.

1855
01:58:35.640 --> 01:58:37.800
Oh, George!

1856
01:58:39.640 --> 01:58:40.680
I'd forgotten that.

1857
01:58:40.760 --> 01:58:43.560
Chew that bacon good and slow.

1858
01:58:43.640 --> 01:58:45.960
It'll help keep you warm on a cold day.

1859
01:58:46.760 --> 01:58:47.920
Ah!

1860
01:58:52.160 --> 01:58:54.400
[WISTFULLY] Oh, Mam.

1861
01:58:58.080 --> 01:59:01.400
Just look at me one minute,
as though you really saw me.

1862
01:59:06.840 --> 01:59:10.920
Mam, 14 years have gone by.

1863
01:59:16.800 --> 01:59:18.440
I'm dead.

1864
01:59:21.240 --> 01:59:24.520
You're a grandmother, Mam.

1865
01:59:25.400 --> 01:59:28.440
I married George Gibbs, Mam.

1866
01:59:32.640 --> 01:59:35.080
Wally's dead, too.

1867
01:59:36.440 --> 01:59:42.200
Mam, his appendix burst
on a camping trip to Penconwy.

1868
01:59:42.280 --> 01:59:45.480
We felt just terrible about it,
don't you remember?

1869
01:59:47.000 --> 01:59:50.640
But just for a moment now,
we're all together.

1870
01:59:50.720 --> 01:59:53.640
Mam, just for a moment...

1871
01:59:55.680 --> 01:59:57.880
..we're happy.

1872
02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:01.840
Let's look at one another.

1873
02:00:01.920 --> 02:00:05.680
That in the yellow paper
is something I found in the attic,

1874
02:00:05.760 --> 02:00:07.800
among your grandmother's things.

1875
02:00:07.880 --> 02:00:11.600
You're old enough to wear it now,
and I thought you'd like it.

1876
02:00:13.680 --> 02:00:15.840
EMILY: And this is from you?

1877
02:00:21.120 --> 02:00:23.000
Why, Mam, it's just lovely.

1878
02:00:25.600 --> 02:00:27.840
And it's just what I wanted.

1879
02:00:31.240 --> 02:00:35.120
– Oh, it's beautiful!
– Well, I hoped you'd like it.

1880
02:00:35.200 --> 02:00:36.880
Hunted all over.

1881
02:00:36.960 --> 02:00:39.040
Your Aunt Norah couldn't find one
in Concord,

1882
02:00:39.120 --> 02:00:41.320
so I had to send all the way to Boston.

1883
02:00:41.400 --> 02:00:43.560
Wally has something for you, too.

1884
02:00:43.640 --> 02:00:47.400
He made it at manual-training class
and he's very proud of it.

1885
02:00:47.480 --> 02:00:50.240
Be sure you make a big fuss about it.

1886
02:00:50.320 --> 02:00:54.680
Your father has a surprise for you, too.
Don't know what it is myself, but...

1887
02:00:56.200 --> 02:00:59.320
Hush, here he comes.

1888
02:00:59.400 --> 02:01:02.360
MR WEBB: Where's my girl?
Where's my birthday girl?

1889
02:01:02.440 --> 02:01:04.200
I can't.

1890
02:01:07.600 --> 02:01:09.440
I can't go on.

1891
02:01:13.440 --> 02:01:16.520
It goes so fast.

1892
02:01:18.240 --> 02:01:20.960
We don't have time to look at one another.

1893
02:01:25.720 --> 02:01:27.200
Oh, I didn't realise.

1894
02:01:31.120 --> 02:01:34.560
So, all that was going on
and we never noticed?

1895
02:01:43.760 --> 02:01:45.840
Take me back.

1896
02:02:02.240 --> 02:02:04.000
EMILY: Up the hill.

1897
02:02:10.360 --> 02:02:12.200
To my grave.

1898
02:02:21.440 --> 02:02:24.400
But first... Wait!

1899
02:02:27.240 --> 02:02:29.280
Just one more look.

1900
02:02:35.000 --> 02:02:36.440
Goodbye.

1901
02:02:37.800 --> 02:02:39.800
Goodbye, world.

1902
02:02:42.480 --> 02:02:44.040
Goodbye...

1903
02:02:45.360 --> 02:02:47.360
Grover's Corners.

1904
02:02:48.840 --> 02:02:50.280
Mam.

1905
02:02:53.880 --> 02:02:55.320
And Dad.

1906
02:02:57.760 --> 02:03:00.720
Goodbye to clocks ticking.

1907
02:03:01.600 --> 02:03:03.800
Oh, and Mam's sunflowers.

1908
02:03:04.800 --> 02:03:06.080
And food.

1909
02:03:07.240 --> 02:03:08.720
Oh, and coffee.

1910
02:03:10.160 --> 02:03:13.800
And new-ironed dresses and hot baths.

1911
02:03:19.240 --> 02:03:21.000
And sleeping.

1912
02:03:25.960 --> 02:03:27.840
And waking up.

1913
02:03:32.560 --> 02:03:34.320
Oh, Earth.

1914
02:03:35.960 --> 02:03:40.600
You are too wonderful
for anybody to realise you.

1915
02:03:42.800 --> 02:03:47.520
Do any human beings ever realise life
while they live it?

1916
02:03:49.640 --> 02:03:51.080
Every...

1917
02:03:53.480 --> 02:03:55.080
Every minute?

1918
02:03:57.160 --> 02:03:59.240
STAGE MANAGER: No.

1919
02:04:00.120 --> 02:04:04.520
The saints and poets, maybe.

1920
02:04:05.360 --> 02:04:07.080
They do.

1921
02:04:07.160 --> 02:04:08.560
Some.

1922
02:04:16.480 --> 02:04:18.160
I'm ready to go back.

1923
02:04:43.880 --> 02:04:46.080
MRS GIBBS: Were you happy?

1924
02:04:49.800 --> 02:04:51.080
No.

1925
02:04:53.480 --> 02:04:55.680
I should have listened to you.

1926
02:04:57.240 --> 02:04:59.560
That's all human beings are.

1927
02:05:01.760 --> 02:05:04.440
Just blind people.

1928
02:05:04.520 --> 02:05:06.680
MRS GIBBS: Look, it's clearing up.

1929
02:05:08.760 --> 02:05:10.400
The stars are coming out.

1930
02:05:10.480 --> 02:05:13.960
Oh, Mr Stimson,
I should have listened to them.

1931
02:05:14.040 --> 02:05:19.280
Yes, now you know. Now you know
that's what it was to be alive.

1932
02:05:20.560 --> 02:05:23.280
To move about in a cloud of ignorance,

1933
02:05:23.360 --> 02:05:26.640
to go up and down trampling
on the feelings of those...

1934
02:05:27.800 --> 02:05:29.240
..of those about you,

1935
02:05:29.320 --> 02:05:33.920
to spend and waste time
as if you had a million years.

1936
02:05:34.000 --> 02:05:38.400
To be always at the mercy
of one self-centred passion or another.

1937
02:05:38.480 --> 02:05:40.720
Now you know!

1938
02:05:40.800 --> 02:05:44.400
That's the happy existence
you wanted to go back to.

1939
02:05:45.720 --> 02:05:47.200
Ignorance and blindness.

1940
02:05:47.280 --> 02:05:51.880
Simon Stimson, that isn't the whole truth,
and you know it.

1941
02:05:52.760 --> 02:05:54.480
[EMILY CRYING]

1942
02:05:56.960 --> 02:05:59.560
Emily, look at that star.

1943
02:06:01.720 --> 02:06:03.680
I forget its name.

1944
02:06:04.280 --> 02:06:08.280
DEAD MAN: My boy, Joel, was a sailor.
He knew 'em all.

1945
02:06:08.960 --> 02:06:12.840
He'd set on the porch evenings
and tell 'em all by name.

1946
02:06:12.920 --> 02:06:16.000
Yes, sir, wonderful!

1947
02:06:16.080 --> 02:06:19.720
DEAD WOMAN: Mm,
a star is mighty good company. Yes.

1948
02:06:20.960 --> 02:06:24.080
– Yes, it is.
– SIMON: Here's one of them coming.

1949
02:06:24.160 --> 02:06:26.720
DEAD WOMAN: There's no time
for one of them to be here.

1950
02:06:26.800 --> 02:06:29.640
– Mother Gibbs, it's George.
– MRS GIBBS: Ssh, dear.

1951
02:06:29.720 --> 02:06:32.520
Just rest yourself.

1952
02:06:35.360 --> 02:06:36.920
It's George.

1953
02:06:37.000 --> 02:06:40.520
DEAD MAN: And my boy,
Joel, who knew the stars, he used to say

1954
02:06:40.600 --> 02:06:44.920
that it took millions of years for that
fleck of light to get to the Earth.

1955
02:06:45.000 --> 02:06:48.280
Don't seem like a body could believe it,
but that's what he used to say.

1956
02:06:49.320 --> 02:06:51.200
Millions of years.

1957
02:06:59.200 --> 02:07:02.160
DEAD WOMAN: Goodness.
That's no way to behave.

1958
02:07:02.240 --> 02:07:04.360
MRS SOAMES: He ought to be home.

1959
02:07:07.160 --> 02:07:09.440
EMILY: Mother Gibbs?

1960
02:07:10.800 --> 02:07:12.480
Yes, Emily?

1961
02:07:14.800 --> 02:07:16.920
They don't understand.

1962
02:07:18.680 --> 02:07:20.240
Do they?

1963
02:07:23.680 --> 02:07:25.200
No, dear.

1964
02:07:27.920 --> 02:07:29.840
They don't understand.

1965
02:07:29.920 --> 02:07:31.840
[BELL TOLLS]

1966
02:08:12.040 --> 02:08:13.840
[BELL STOPS]

1967
02:08:19.440 --> 02:08:22.280
STAGE MANAGER: Most everybody's asleep
in Grover's Corners.

1968
02:08:25.760 --> 02:08:27.680
There are a few lights on.

1969
02:08:30.520 --> 02:08:35.680
Shorty Hawkins, down at the depot,

1970
02:08:35.760 --> 02:08:38.320
just watched the Albany train go by.

1971
02:08:39.960 --> 02:08:41.800
And at the livery stable.

1972
02:08:43.000 --> 02:08:46.320
Someone's setting up late and talking.

1973
02:08:53.840 --> 02:08:56.760
Yes, it's clearing up.

1974
02:08:59.160 --> 02:09:01.400
There are the stars –

1975
02:09:01.480 --> 02:09:06.440
doing their old,
old crisscross journeys in the sky.

1976
02:09:08.680 --> 02:09:11.560
Scholars haven't settled the matter yet,

1977
02:09:11.640 --> 02:09:14.960
but they seem to think
there's no living beings up there.

1978
02:09:16.560 --> 02:09:18.760
Just chalk.

1979
02:09:18.840 --> 02:09:20.320
Or fire.

1980
02:09:22.320 --> 02:09:25.240
Only this one is straining away.

1981
02:09:28.160 --> 02:09:33.080
Straining away all the time
to make something of itself.

1982
02:09:36.280 --> 02:09:41.800
The strain's so bad, every 16 hours
everybody lies down and gets a rest.

1983
02:09:45.360 --> 02:09:47.880
Eleven o'clock in Grover's Corners.

1984
02:09:50.160 --> 02:09:52.640
You get a good rest, too.

1985
02:09:54.080 --> 02:09:55.760
Good night.

1986
02:09:59.320 --> 02:10:01.200
(APPLAUSE, CHEERS)
